\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  seen hurting yen's status. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2003\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 17 (Reuters) - Tokyo's relentless campaign of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is putting market players off the yen and threatening to reduce the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to minority status, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  So far this year, Japanese authorities have spent about 7.0 trillion yen ($59.26 billion) to stem the yen's rise, which they fear could hurt exports and hinder the country's fragile economic recovery. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) data disclosed in May showed the authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on 17 days during the January-March period. \par \par \uc2  Traders say all the interference has turned the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a pariah. \par \par \uc2  "For the past few months, people have simply refrained from trading dollar/yen...they don't want to touch the yen," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Alan Greenspan has certainly taken note. \par \par \uc2  During the question-and-answer session after his Tuesday testimony on Capitol Hill, the chairman of the Federal Reserve said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not go on indefinitely. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say, however, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the yen below 115 yen  against the dollar - it was around 118 yen on Thursday - until share prices rise more sharply and the domestic economy shows convincing signs of recovery. \par \par \uc2  But the prolonged control is chasing away traders and could result in slimmed down yen trading. \par \par \uc2  "Without the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar could be well below 110 yen by now," said Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly dollar/yen is being heavily managed...at the expense of reducing (dollar/yen) volatility," Saito said. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen implied options volatility has hit lows around 8.0-9.0 percent in the past two months and has been mostly below 10 percent from the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities might favour low volatility because it implies the market is calm and stable, but it also means that there is less activity, which makes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  less attractive for dealers, traders say. \par \par \uc2  BLUNTED IMPACT \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary officials, without admitting they have been in the market in July, argue that they are working to prevent speculative moves. \par \par \uc2  They say such moves are bad for the yen as it hinders the activities of operators, primarily exporters and importers, who need to buy or sell dollars for actual use. \par \par \uc2  Last week, Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the MOF's international bureau, told reporters: "It is best for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move stably so that players can predict movements." \par \par \uc2  But in recent weeks, dealers say they've witnessed sudden and unnatural rises in the dollar, prompting speculation about further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was most recently suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in post-Asian trade on Tuesday when the dollar dropped to a near two-month low of 116.65 yen. \par \par \uc2  Rumours have also circulated that Japan may have sold the yen in Asia on Wednesday, triggering a wave of stop-loss buying around 118.00 and 118.20 yen that boosted the dollar to a two-week high of 118.70, dealers say. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers say, have the greatest impact when there is an element of surprise. \par \par \uc2  The market was shocked when the BOJ first took stealthy action in January, and the effect lasted until March. \par \par \uc2  But the repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dulled the impact. \par \par \uc2  "The yen now looks like a fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and certainly doesn't look like a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that belongs to the Group of Seven nations anymore," said the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager. \par \par \uc2  The dollar/yen rate has floated in a range of just about seven yen, moving between 115 and 121.88 yen so far in 2003. \par \par \uc2  If that trend holds through the end of the year, it would be the narrowest range since Japan floated the yen in 1973. ($1=118.11 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030717dz7h00a6w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
