\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar firms on US economic hopes, DMZ shooting. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang \par \uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2003\par 05:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 17 (Reuters) - The dollar nudged up against the yen and euro on Thursday, drawing support from both foreign and Japanese players who saw better prospects for the U.S. economy. Dealers said the dollar was also rescued from the day's lows on falling Tokyo stocks and news that South Korea had exchanged machinegun fire with North Korea in the demilitarised zone. \par \par \uc2  But trading was mostly confined to narrow ranges because the second leg of Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's congressional testimony on Wednesday failed to offer new incentives and the market waited for more U.S. data for clues on the nation's economic future. \par \par \uc2  "Dealers want to see more U.S. indicators to assess whether the economic recovery is for real," said Kouki Muroi, deputy manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading group at Aozora Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Right now, expectations are getting ahead of reality." \par \par \uc2  Greenspan largely reiterated what he had said the previous day, noting that the U.S. economy was recovering. \par \par \uc2  He also vowed the Fed would remain vigilant on deflation. \par \par \uc2  The market was taking a wait-and-see stance after the greenback fell from a two-month high of $1.1109 hit on Wednesday against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The shooting on the Korean peninsula was between observations posts on either side of the demarcation line at the heart of the DMZ. \par \par \uc2  South Korea will carry out an investigation and based on the results would determine the next step, said Colonel Lee Hong-ki, spokesman for the South's Joint Chiefs of Staff. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR FIRM AGAINST YEN \par \par \uc2  The dollar was solid against the yen as the Japanese monetary authorities were thought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  most recently on Tuesday when it dropped below 117 yen for the first time since late May, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A rumour circulated that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Asian time on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is highly unlikely to tumble against the yen as the authorities seem to be showing strong commitment to restraining a sharp rise in the yen," said Michihisa Tanimoto, deputy general manager at Sumitomo Life Insurance. \par \par \uc2  "Amid this trend, speculators cannot sell the dollar heavily, which should make it solid from around 117 yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Still, the greenback faced some selling pressure after Greenspan commented during the question and answer session at Wednesday's testimony on the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying it could not be done indefinitely. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts expect the dollar to remain trapped in the recent 117.50-118.50 yen range for now because the downside is firmly supported by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the upside is capped by resistance on exporter offers. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, continued to offer verbal threats, saying on Thursday there was still some volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and repeated that the Japanese authorities were ready to take all appropriate measures. \par \par \uc2  At 0250 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 118.17/22 yen off from the day's low of 117.88 yen and compares with Wednesday's late U.S. level of 118.07/15. \par \par \uc2  The euro was little changed at $1.1216/21 from its U.S. level of $1.1220/24, with a wait-and-see mood dominanting. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke was quoted as saying he would not bet on any further rate cuts from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment because they would not have any positive economic effect. \par \par \uc2  "It's still tough to buy euros looking both at economic fundamentals and charts," said Sumitomo Life's Tanimoto. \par \par \uc2  "It's more likely the euro will test below $1.1 in the near term," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030717dz7h006hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
