\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Net Portfolio Investment Inflow To Japan Soars \b0\par\par\uc2  By Asako Tanabe \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2003\par 10:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Securities investment into Japan surged to a net Y2.037 trillion last week, the highest level since investment flow data were first published in April 2001. \par \par \uc2  The massive inflow matches a strong recovery in Japan's bond market and an 11-month peak in the country's share market last week although some analysts say the flow of portfolio investment funds may have peaked. \par \par \uc2  Japan's buoyant markets not only attracted foreign investors but also led Japanese investors to repatriate funds from offshore markets. \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors sold a net Y1.304 trillion of foreign bonds and a net Y68.7 billion of foreign stocks in the week that ended July 11, according to data issued Thursday by Japan's Finance Ministry. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, foreign investors turned net buyers of Japanese stocks and bonds - buying a net Y556.1 billion of the former and Y108.0 billion of the latter. \par \par \uc2  The flood into Japan's securities markets last week follows a Y538.3 billion inflow the previous week and is a sharp turnaround from continuous outflows between mid-February and mid-June. \par \par \uc2  But analysts say the investment into Japan may slow. \par \par \uc2  "We expect that this is likely near the peak...as Japanese sales of foreign bonds are likely to taper off and foreign buying of Japanese equities has turned around," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the Nikkei 225 Stock Average ended 2.4% lower today at 9498.86 and is down 5.6% from its intraday peak a week ago of 10070.11. Foreign investors were also net sellers of Japanese stocks in pre-market orders for the second successive day Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The inflows have meant the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has likely had to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the yen rising. \par \par \uc2  "This morning's data showed a massive capital inflow, with Japanese foreign bond selling, and foreigners continuing to buy Japanese equities," said Tomoko Fujii, head of economic and market analysis at Nikko Citigroup. "The Japanese authorities needed to find something to offset this - their answer is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants say the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely has sold up to Y600 billion so far this month, having already sold about Y7 trillion in the first half of 2003. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Securities Investment Flow Table\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The figures represent billions of yen. The minus sign indicates net\par selling:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                       July 7-   June 30-\par                                            11    July 4\par Japan Gross Purchases Overseas Bonds   2,216.9   2,127.9\par             Gross Sells                3,521.0   2,284.6\par                 Net                   -1,304.1    -156.7\par Japan Gross Purchases Overseas Shares    276.0     186.7\par             Gross Sells                  344.7     214.3\par                 Net                      -68.7     -27.6\par Foreign Gross Purchases Japan Bonds    1,608.3   1,440.5r\par             Gross Sells                1,500.3   1,716.0r\par                 Net                      108.0    -275.5r\par Foreign Gross Purchases Japan Shares   2,373.4   2,258.0\par      