\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar eyes higher ground after Greenspan leg-up. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2003\par 10:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 16 (Reuters) - The dollar strode to its highest in over two months against the euro, Swiss franc and sterling on Wednesday, extending gains after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan delivered an upbeat message on U.S. growth prospects. \par \par \uc2  Strong earnings from tech bellwether Intel Corp, reported after the U.S. stock exchange closed, also fired the dollar, reinforcing the brighter picture painted by Tuesday's strong U.S. consumer spending and regional industry figures. \par \par \uc2  The dollar pushed as high as $1.1115 to the euro in early trade, extending Tuesday's one percent rally to hit its strongest level since April 30. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing follow-through from Greenspan after his uncharacteristically upbeat comments on the economy," said Mitul Kotecha, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "Greenspan's upbeat outlook and positive earnings releases provide fuel for an ongoing shift out of bonds and into equities, which is positive for the dollar." \par \par \uc2  Government bonds sold off sharply after Greenspan told Congress on Tuesday that the U.S. economy "could very well be embarking on a period of extended growth". \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock markets succumbed to a bout of profit-taking but were set for a strong open on Wednesday after tech giant Intel reported a doubling of its second-quarter net earnings. \par \par \uc2  BIDDING DOLLAR HIGHER \par \par \uc2  The dollar also firmed against the yen as talk of yen-selling by Japanese authorities triggered a wave of stop-loss dollar buying, lifting the greenback towards a two-week high of 118.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on several days in the past week, fearing appreciation in the yen could harm the country's fragile export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the BOJ did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight but I don't think the volumes were particularly large," said one London-based trader. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar was already on the way up." \par \par \uc2  The market showed muted reaction to the Bank of Japan upgrading its assessment of the economy for the first time in a year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its monthly economic report that, although economic activity remained flat, it no longer saw signs of weakness in exports. The euro, which has already lost more than six yen against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, was little changed on the day at 131.74 yen. \par \par \uc2  CANADIAN DOLLAR HURT \par \par \uc2  The recent trend of pulling funds out of high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  back into the dollar gathered pace after a surprise 25 basis point cut from the Bank of Canada on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar fell to a nine-week low beyond C$1.3960 to the greenback after the cut and also hit a two-month low against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The general dollar-buying trend intensified, especially after the Bank of Canada's surprise rate cut, which prompted further selling of high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the corporate desk at Trust and Custody Services Bank. \par \par \uc2  "All this improved the (dollar-buying) mood which had already been lifted by Greenspan and the U.S. data," Furumaya said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030716dz7g00go9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
