\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Declines Against Euro, Pound, Advances vs. Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2 1,229 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2003\par 23:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- After whipsaw trading, the dollar ended mostly lower Wednesday as investors struggled to find direction in a nervous \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the euro was trading at $1.1208, up from $1.1188 late Tuesday. The dollar was trading at 118.07 yen, up from 117.79 yen late Tuesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was buying 1.3755 Swiss francs, down from 1.3825 francs on Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.5940, up from $1.5912 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Early in the New York session, the dollar had pulled back from multimonth highs against major counterparts, coinciding with a midmorning rebound for U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  The dollar thereby reversed some of its gains from its stunning rally in the wake of Tuesday's optimistic congressional testimony by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  The rebound Wednesday in New York for the euro, Swiss franc and sterling as well as commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Canadian and Australian dollars suggested investors are still undecided whether prospects for growth or higher yields will reign supreme in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The burst of strength the dollar has shown in the past few weeks was interpreted by some analysts as an indication that investors were shifting their focus to the potential for immediate economic growth and away from interest-rate differentials. \par \par \uc2  However, the volatile intraday movements seen Wednesday are evidence the dollar's rally can be easily derailed, traders said. Following mediocre morning U.S. economic data on consumer prices and business inventories, the dollar began a slow grind downwards that accelerated as trading progressed, but then changed course again once active European trading ended. \par \par \uc2  "Given the degree of the move in the euro and the bond market over the last month and a half ... the market's really assessing whether we've hit a short-term bottom in the euro around $1.11," said Meg Browne, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. "[The moves are] much bigger than [Wednesday's] data." \par \par \uc2  Early in the global session, the dollar succeeded in pushing the euro to three-month lows of $1.1108, flirting with the key $1.1080 support level and building on Tuesday's gains of more than a cent against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, the euro spiked higher around midday in New York, peaking at $1.1232. When the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to break decisively through key resistance around $1.1230, it quickly retreated to its level from late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also leapt to 1.3938 Swiss francs against the Swiss franc and pushed sterling down to the $1.5851, but then retraced its advance during the New York morning. \par \par \uc2  Some traders felt the market is still ill at ease with a rapidly appreciating dollar and is casting about for more guidance for future moves. \par \par \uc2  "After such a large pullback, the euro may be basing here, there seems to be some support around this level," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Putnam Investments in Boston. "[We may be] getting into a [trading range] before the dollar decline resumes." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's continued rally against the yen, against which it had fallen to a seven-week low on Tuesday, was attributed largely to massive covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BOJ was thought to have bought dollars through commercial banks to halt the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide against yen after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped under 117 yen. \par \par \uc2  Much of the euro's vigor was attributed to a substantial rally in euro-zone bonds, which perked up on the strength on comments from Bundesbank chief Ernst Welteke, a member of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governing council. \par \par \uc2  "Further interest-rate cuts would probably have no positive impact," Mr. Welteke told the German newspaper Das Bild. \par \par \uc2  Comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow that initially helped the dollar earlier in the session appeared to have little lasting impact. Mr. Snow, speaking to London's Times newspaper, described the U.S. economy as "coiled like a spring" and dismissed fears about the size of the U.S. current account gap, saying it wasn't something to lose sleep over. \par \par \uc2  After his assertion on Tuesday that economic growth in the U.S. could be around the corner, Mr. Greenspan's testimony on Wednesday seemed to hold little resonance for the market, with the dollar continuing to sell off as he spoke. \par \par \uc2  However, Mr. Greenspan couldn't escape more questions about the Chinese yuan's relation to the dollar. According to the Fed chairman, Beijing is going to have to consider floating the yuan at some point. \par \par \uc2  "From an economic point of view it's going to become increasingly evident that is going to have to happen, if existing cost structures around the world remain what they are," he said in response to a question. \par \par \uc2  China tightly controls the flow of capital across its borders, allowing only partial convertibility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the capital account and has held the yuan steady since the mid-1990s at about 8.30 yuan to the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading on the current account is tightly monitored, with the central People's Bank of China the dominant participant in the mainland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The Chinese authorities won't be able to accumulate huge amounts of dollar assets indefinitely "because it will create an inability of their monetary system to function well," Mr. Greenspan noted. \par \par \uc2  When asked about the need to pressure the Chinese government on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, Mr. Greenspan said a number of China's trading partners had raised the issue of the artificially low yuan. \par \par \uc2  "The question is whether or not firm public discussion moves the ball further down the road than private discussion," Mr. Greenspan said. \par \par \uc2  The number of questions Mr. Greenspan received -- as well as his willingness to answer them -- has given the controversy on China's virtual peg of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar an even higher profile. On earlier occasions, U.S. officials, including Treasury Secretary Snow, have commented on the need for more flexibility in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  "Recently, the U.S. manufacturing lobby has started raising the issue of the China peg more strongly than ever before and hence the senators are getting more and more aware of this," said Anindya Chatterjee, emerging-market strategist at IDEAglobal.com in New York. "They're striking a populist note and raising the issue in public." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was unable to find any solace among the so-called commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the Canadian, Australian and New Zealand dollars all rallied on the back of the euro's newfound strength, and despite worries that the Bank of Canada's decision to cut its key overnight target to 3.00% on Tuesday might continue to send reverberations through the bloc. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the Australian dollar was buying 65.56 U.S. cents, up from 64.90 cents late Tuesday. Against the Canadian dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying $1.3845 Canadian dollars, down from $1.3907 late Tuesday, while the New Zealand dollar was buying 58.31 U.S. cents, up from 57.99 cents. \par \par \uc2  --Mirela Vlad in Paris and Robert Flint and Grainne McCarthy in New York contributed to this article. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  07-16-03 1754ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030716dz7g007k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
