\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Steadies Vs Euro As Mkt Searches For Direction \b0\par\par\uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2003\par 19:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Michael Deibert\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- After some flip-flop moves in earlier global\par trading, the dollar is little changed versus the euro Wednesday\par afternoon as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market struggles for direction.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  During the New York morning session, the dollar had pulled backed from multi-months highs against most of its major counterparts, coinciding with the midmorning rebound for U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  The dollar thereby reversed some its gains after a stunning rally in the wake of Tuesday's optimistic congressional testimony by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  The earlier rebound Wednesday in New York for the euro, Swiss franc and sterling as well as commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Canadian and Australian dollars suggested investors are still undecided whether prospects for growth or higher yields will reign supreme in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Given the degree of the move in the euro and the bond market over the last month and a half...The market's really assessing whether we've hit a short term bottom in the euro around $1.11," said Meg Browne, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist  with HSBC in New York. "(The moves are) much bigger than (Wednesday's) data." \par \par \uc2  Early in the global session, the dollar succeeded in pushing the euro to three- month lows of $1.1108, flirting with the key $1.1080 support level and building on Tuesday gains of more than a cent against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, the euro spiked higher around midday in New York, peaking at $1.1232. When the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to break decisively through key resistance at around $1.1230, it quickly retreated to its level from late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also leapt to CHF1.3938 against the Swiss franc and pushed sterling down to the $1.5851, but had pared  its advance during the New York morning. \par \par \uc2  Some traders felt the market is still ill at ease with a rapidly appreciating dollar and is casting about for more guidance for future moves. \par \par \uc2  "After such a large pullback, the euro may be basing here, there seems to be some support around this level," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Putnam Investments in Boston. "(We may be) getting into a (trading range) before the dollar decline resumes." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's continued rally against the yen, against which it had fallen to a seven-week low on Tuesday, was attributed largely to massive covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BOJ was thought to have bought dollars through commercial banks to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide against yen after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped under Y117.00. \par \par \uc2  Following mediocre morning U.S. economic data on  consumer prices and business inventories, the dollar began a slow grind downwards that accelerated as trading progressed, but then reversed course once active European trading ended. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday afternoon, the euro was trading at $1.1192,  little changed from $1.1188 late Tuesday. The dollar was trading at Y118.12, up from Y117.79 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc the dollar was buying CHF1.3763,  down from CHF1.3825 on Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.5933, down from $1.5912 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  After his assertion that economic growth in the U.S. could be around the corner on Tuesday, Greenspan's testimony on Wednesday seemed to hold little resonance for the market, with the dollar continuing to sell off pace as he spoke. \par \par \uc2  Much of the euro's newfound vigor was attributed a substantial rally in euro-zone bonds, which perked up on the strength on comments from Bundesbank chief Ernst Welteke, a  member of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governing council. \par \par \uc2  "Further interest-rate cuts would probably have no positive impact," Welteke told the German newspaper Das Bild. \par \par \uc2  Comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow that initially helped the dollar earlier in the session appeared to have little lasting impact. Snow, speaking to London's Times newspaper,  described the U.S. economy as "coiled like a spring" and dismissed fears about the size of the U.S. current account gap, saying it wasn't something to lose sleep over. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires, \par \par \uc2  201-938-2248, email:michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Mirela Vlad in Paris contributed to this article) \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030716dz7g0017d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
