\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Sharply Higher On Talk Japan MOF Sold Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Twaronite \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,070 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2003\par 07:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently didn't want the yen to be left behind Wednesday, after overnight upbeat remarks from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan pushed the dollar up against the euro but failed to lift the dollar against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The ministry appears to have followed up overnight yen sales with further selling this morning in Tokyo, traders said, which sent the dollar to a high of Y118.68 at 0014 GMT from its session low of Y117.85 a mere 48 minutes earlier. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking by U.S. funds and Japanese exporter sales pared those gains in the afternoon, but the perception that the ministry would act again kept market participants from testing the dollar's downside. \par \par \uc2  If it weren't for talk that the Finance Ministry was selling yen through the Bank of Japan, many market participants said the dollar likely would have remained under pressure against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Markets move on expectations, and the yen is in a strengthening trend because markets recognize Japan's potential to improve," said Ryohei Muramatsu, treasury manager at Commerzbank in Tokyo. "Japan's economic outlook is brighter, stocks are rising, so there is no reason for the dollar to strengthen - there are only reasons for it to weaken" against the yen, he said. \par \par \uc2  That explains why the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a seven-week low of Y116.65 overnight, he said, before talk of the ministry's covert yen sales pushed it back up. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan declared Tuesday that the U.S. "stands ready to maintain a highly accommodative stance of policy for as long as it takes to achieve a return to satisfactory economic performance," and cited "tentative signs" that investment might be picking up. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, Greenspan said while recent data from Japan "have seemed somewhat less negative," concerns remain. "As has been true for some time, Japan's economy remains in difficult straits, burdened by a weak banking sector and an ongoing deflation," he said. \par \par \uc2  His comments were likely not intended as tacit support for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a strong yen from harming the nation's fragile economy. But they did indicate that the U.S.' assessment of Japan's economic situation doesn't differ greatly from that of Japanese financial officials. \par \par \uc2  "Our stance is unchanged that we will take steps, as necessary, against what are seen as speculative moves," Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi said Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  While Mizoguchi refused to comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not, he told reporters: "I think that somewhat speculative moves were seen" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, implying that the ministry did indeed act against such moves. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y118.35, above Y117.79 in New York and Y117.69 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1148, lower than $1.1188 in New York and $1.1266 in Tokyo. Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y131.94, compared with Y131.78 in New York and Y132.59 in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.5907, compared with $1.5901 in New York and $1.6135 in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also underpinned by news that the Bank of Japan upgraded its economic assessment for the first time since July 2002 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said uncertainty over the economy is abating slightly and that exports were flat as opposed to weak. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained caution about the state of the economy going forward due to ongoing uncertainty over the economic recovery overseas, mainly in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The euro lost more ground in the morning as yen-selling talk pushed up the dollar-yen. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a new three-month low of $1.1115 at 0022 GMT, before short-covering pared losses. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan will make another congressional appearance Wednesday to testify before the Senate Banking Committee. \par \par \uc2  Market participants are also keeping their eyes on another horizon, as U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow is on a four-day visit to the U.K. and Germany to meet with financial officials. \par \par \uc2  Snow told The Times of London in an interview published on the newspaper's website Wednesday that the 12 eurozone countries should take stronger measures to revive their economies. \par \par \uc2  He said he was "very confident" that U.S. growth would accelerate strongly from an annual rate of 3% in the present quarter to "comfortably above 4%" next year, and that the first signs of this were "already beginning to be seen," with improving business confidence, rising profits, a stable dollar, and a big expected influx of foreign investment. \par \par \uc2  And in case European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials had any ideas about following Japan's example if the euro were to retest highs, Snow pre-emptively said that governments should best leave \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to markets. \par \par \uc2  "In America and Europe we have a freely floating competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system and governments that don't believe in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." he said. "If we manage our economies well, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take care of themselves." \par \par \uc2  Some Asian strategists wondered how long before such U.S. rumblings against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general turn to specific complaints about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy to keep the yen from strengthening. \par \par \uc2  "The Bush administration wants to see a healthy economic recovery before the (2004 U.S. presidential) election, meaning they need reasonable growth early next year," and might take aim at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions, said Credit Suisse First Boston strategist Satoru Ogasawara. \par \par \uc2  For now, though, he said CSFB will likely maintain its three-month dollar forecast of Y117. \par \par \uc2  "Downward pressure on the dollar remains intact, but as we've seen last night, Japanese authorities will try to maintain the dollar-yen around Y117, and so far they've been succeeding," he said. \par \par \uc2  -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by John Gibson \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 