\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Rallies on Positive Data, Fed Chairman's Testimony \b0\par\par\uc2 1,079 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2003\par 00:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -- On the strength of a one-two punch from positive economic\par data and upbeat congressional testimony from Federal Reserve Chairman\par Alan Greenspan, the dollar surged broadly against its rivals Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Aided by profit-taking as well as euro sales against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar found enough support to resume the climb it began in June. \par \par \uc2  During a volatile stretch of New York trading, the dollar gained about a cent against the euro, pushing the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a three-month low at $1.1156 from a session high of $1.1335, while leaping to 1.3869 Swiss francs against the Swiss franc from a low of 1.3681 francs. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday, the euro was trading at $1.1188, down sharply from $1.1285 late Monday. Against the Swiss franc the dollar was buying 1.3825 francs, up steeply from 1.36737 francs on Monday. The pound sterling, still feeling the effects of the Bank of England's rate cut last week, was also pummeled, falling to $1.5901 from $1.6140 in the course of the session. Sterling was at $1.5912, down from $1.6122 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to 116.67 yen against the yen, a seven-week low, before popping back up to the 117.70 yen level on suspected covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Late Tuesday, the dollar was trading at 117.79 yen against the Japanese yen, up from 117.63 late Monday yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also surged to a two-month high against its Canadian counterpart, as the Canadian dollar sank following a surprise decision by the Bank of Canada to cut its key overnight target rate by a quarter percentage point to 3.00%. The U.S. dollar was trading at $1.3915 Canadian dollars late Tuesday, up steeply from C$1.3751 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT) and from C$1.3744 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Another victim of the dollar's rally was the Australian dollar, which tumbled to a five-week low of 64.84 U.S. cents from its close Monday at 65.90 cents, before coming to rest at the 65.08 cents level late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Greenspan's declaration that growth in the U.S. economy could be around the corner came on the heels of Commerce Department figures released Tuesday showing that U.S. retail sales rose in June, 0.5% for the month, the biggest monthly increase since March, and the Federal Reserve Bank of New York's Empire State Manufacturing Survey for July, which slipped to a reading of 22.6 from 27.6 in June, though the number was still regarded as a high one. \par \par \uc2  "There was a pretty optimistic forecast [in Greenspan's report]," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Morgan Stanley in New York. "There was nothing to dissuade markets of the reflation theme, and that's dollar supportive." \par \par \uc2  An emphasis on growth has gained momentum in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as recent surges in global equities and declines in global sovereigns has many investors reconsidering their asset allocations in favor of instruments that offer growth potential rather than higher yield, noted Jana Butland, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with FleetBoston Financial in Boston. \par \par \uc2  "It seems like its a question of whether this could be an equity or bond market story going forward," Ms. Butland said. \par \par \uc2  While the dollar gained, U.S. Treasurys suffered one of their biggest one-day selloffs this year after Mr. Greenspan damped the market's hope for unconventional monetary-easing measures such as buying Treasurys notes. By talking up the economy's prospects while maintaining a highly stimulative monetary policy and discounting the specter of deflation, Mr. Greenspan delivered bad news for investors owning long-dated Treasurys, sparking a clear rush out of that segment of the yield curve. \par \par \uc2  Longer-dated maturities suffered the most losses and the Treasury yield curve -- or difference between short- and long-dated maturities -- steepened significantly. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, U.S. stocks ended lower after a choppy session, with equity investors apparently in need of more confirmation that the U.S. economy is indeed poised to do better. The Dow Jones Industrial Average finished down 0.5% at 9128 and the Nasdaq Composite Index slipped 0.1% at 1753. \par \par \uc2  The only holdout among the dollar's major rivals was the Japanese yen, stubbornly continuing to appreciate following a decision by the Bank of Japan to leave monetary policy unchanged at the conclusion of a two-day meeting, and despite comments by Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, that Japanese economic fundamentals did not warrant a strengthening yen. \par \par \uc2  In cutting rates, the Bank of Canada cited slowing of near-term domestic economic activity because of effects of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome, or SARS, and an isolated case of Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy, or mad-cow disease, in Canada and weaker foreign demand for Canadian products. \par \par \uc2  "We saw the same thing from the Bank of England [last week]," said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with J.P. Morgan in New York. "Central banks that were earlier this year more tolerant of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength are now being forced to cut rates because the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have damped both growth and inflation." \par \par \uc2  Last week, the Bank of England lowered its key rate a quarter percentage to 3.50%, the first rate cut since February. The BOE cited a "hesitant" global economy, a weaker outlook for U.K. external demand and below-trend growth. \par \par \uc2  Also on Tuesday, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow told reporters in London that he didn't expect European monetary officials to bring up during his European trip the issue of the euro's recent appreciation against the dollar or the effect the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have on exports and economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The euro-zone economies "didn't have much growth two years ago when the euro was weaker," Mr. Snow said. "It goes deeper than the dollar-euro relationship." \par \par \uc2  Mr. Snow arrived in London Tuesday for meetings with bankers and government officials. On Thursday, Mr. Snow will travel on to Frankfurt for further meetings. \par \par \uc2  Write to Michael Deibert at michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  --Don Curren in Toronto, Elizabeth Price in London and Simona Covel contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  07-15-03 1839ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030715dz7f0086h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
