\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Posts Big Gains Against Sterling, Flat Vs. Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 989 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2003\par 23:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro fell moderately against the dollar Monday to finish the\par session under $1.13, putting the buck on solid footing ahead of key U.S.\par economic data and congressional testimony from Federal Reserve Chairman\par Alan Greenspan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  In late trading in New York, the dollar was trading at 117.63 yen, off its global session low of 117.31 yen but little changed from 117.64 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.1285, up from its low of $1.1243 but down from $1.1304 late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6122, down sharply from $1.6300. \par \par \uc2  Sluggish trade typical of the summer months helped keep the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in tight ranges for much of the session, breaking out intermittently and briefly before settling back into well-worn channels. \par \par \uc2  Investors occasionally pushed the dollar lower against the yen to the 117.30 yen area, but such attempts were quickly met by buying that many in the market suspected represented covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts on behalf of the Ministry, has spent trillions of yen this year to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak. In stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank places its dollar-buy orders with large financial institutions rather than announcing its intentions, a strategy that has helped weed out speculators. \par \par \uc2  The euro, meanwhile, looked poised to break out of its near-term range when it breached $1.1250 briefly during the global session, but the momentum that has carried the European single-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down over the last six weeks petered out, allowing the euro to bounce back toward $1.13 during New York trading. \par \par \uc2  Even triple-digit gains over much of the session for the Dow Jones Industrial Average failed to spark a big reaction the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The index pared back some its gains late in the session, finishing well below its highs for the day. \par \par \uc2  Despite the brisk trading in equity markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors mostly huddled on the sidelines, awaiting fresh data due out Tuesday. Along with Mr. Greenspan's testimony, national retail-sales figures and a survey of New York manufacturing activity will be scrutinized for further insights on the direction of the economy. \par \par \uc2  The big exception to the day's pattern was the sharp decline in sterling after investors exited positions amid fears of further rate cuts in the United Kingdom, as well as upcoming testimony from that nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief. \par \par \uc2  His talk this week is billed as the main event for many investors who will be on the lookout for any surprises, such as a more-optimistic economic outlook from the chairman, which would boost the dollar's standing against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  further. \par \par \uc2  Tim Stewart, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York, said, "The market is looking for something fairly balanced" and reminiscent of what the Fed said at its last policy meeting when it disappointed markets by lowering short term interest rates by a quarter-percentage point rather than a half-point. \par \par \uc2  But any comments that support a low long-dated Treasury yields and emphasize a sluggish economy could "take a little bit of the sting out of the dollar" strength and offer the euro a respite from the five-week selling spree that has sent it from $1.1930 to below $1.13, said Mr. Stewart. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Greenspan, however, isn't the only central banker that will grab \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants' attention on Tuesday. The Bank of England governor Mervyn King and Monetary Policy Committee member Rachel Lomax will address Parliament during London trading hours. Last week the BOE surprised markets with a quarter-point cut in overnight rate to 3.5% in its first meeting led by Mr. King. \par \par \uc2  Any remarks on the rate cut and/or views on the potential for more reductions in the pipeline will be of particular interest to investors who accelerated their selling of sterling during New York trade Monday. \par \par \uc2  The downward pressure on the pound for much of the global session eventually forced it through key support levels of $1.6225 and $1.6180, causing some capitulation among investors already wary of holding sterling ahead of Mr. King's testimony, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  As for the Bank of Canada's interest-rate decision due Tuesday, a solid majority of 12 out of 14 surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires now forecast the Bank of Canada will leave interest rates steady. The announcement on interest rates is expected at 9:30 a.m. EDT. \par \par \uc2  A number of negatives facing the Canadian economy -- including the surging Canadian dollar and the second outbreak in May of severe acute respiratory syndrome, or SARS, in Toronto -- had some analysts convinced Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut rates by a quarter-point. \par \par \uc2  But news that Canada's economy created an entirely unforeseen 49,000 jobs in June, combined with a recent softening in the Canadian dollar, have persuaded most local economists the bank will continue to remain on the policy sidelines. \par \par \uc2  In other news, the People's Bank of China Governor Zhou Xiaochuan reaffirmed China's strong and stable monetary policy in speech posted on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Web site, saying its effective implementation has supported economic growth and financial stability. \par \par \uc2  His remarks at a closed \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conference again stress that China plans to resist international pressure to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and instead work at its own pace to introduce \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms. \par \par \uc2  "[China] should continue to promote reform to marketize interest rates, maintain the fundamental stability of the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, and continue to perfect the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate system," he said. \par \par \uc2  Write to Agnes T. Crane at agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Owen Brown in Beijing, Don Curren in Toronto and Michael Deibert in New York contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  07-14-03 1759ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030714dz7e006bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
