\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -2: Dlr Rises, But Japan Exporters Cap At Y118 \b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2003\par 08:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Schroeder's reported remarks about euro strength came hours after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its key interest rate at 2%, as market participants had expected. \par \par \uc2  The dollar benefitted from the euro's fall, and ticked higher against the yen in cross-trading, although sales by Japanese exporters kept the dollar from rising above Y118, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  got some support from morning comments from Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, who said the yen is currently strong with regard to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  counterparts in view of the nation's purchasing power. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not saying that's good or bad, but that's the current situation," Shiokawa told a regular press conference. He declined to comment on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels or market conditions. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa has mentioned purchasing power parity on several occasions since late last year, when he declared that the yen, which was trading near Y125 to the dollar at the time, was "too strong," and that a more appropriate level would be Y150. \par \par \uc2  While Shiokawa's comments Friday helped underpin dollar sentiment, they "weren't a big trading factor today," said a dealer at a Japanese bank, as market participants no longer think that Japan has any intention of selling yen to achieve Shiokawa's theoretical levels. \par \par \uc2  But at the same time, traders believe Japan's finance ministry wouldn't hesitate to sell yen to prevent any sharp appreciation. The government has spent some Y7 trillion since the start of the year \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep a strong yen from undermining \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy. \par \par \uc2  -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Hugh Lawson \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030711dz7b000bk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
