\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Global Officials Sing Weak  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Shuffle \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 1,091 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2003\par 21:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- After a week of surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cuts and marked increase in verbal efforts to influence exchange rates, policymakers around the world seem to be singing exactly the same song: it's called the weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shuffle. \par \par \uc2  With the global economy still looking pretty anemic and the dollar in the midst of a secular decline, the last thing the bulk of U.S. trading partners want right now is a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would kick their competitiveness in global markets. \par \par \uc2  This desire is perhaps most blatantly evident in Japan, where monetary authorities have made no secret of their aim to stand in the way of yen strength, by spending billions of dollars if necessary in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it's also happening elsewhere. Since the Federal Reserve's quarter-percentage-point rate cut last month, the central banks of the U.K., South Korea, Thailand, Taiwan, the Philippines, Indonesia and Hong Kong have all lowered their benchmark lending rates. Singapore also effectively eased monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  While clearly the rate cuts represent efforts to stimulate domestic demand, they are also aimed at reducing the appeal of holding their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bidding them higher at a time when the dollar is weak. \par \par \uc2  "If it's not beggar thy neighbor policies, it's like saying `I don't want my neighbors to follow beggar thy neighbor policies," said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast financial consultancy in New York. "In an environment where nobody has particularly strong growth, nobody feels any comfort in having their monetary conditions tightened by having a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par       Euro Steps Into Breach\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par What's more, with deflation already a factor in some large economies -\par and a clear and present threat in others -  policymakers are going to be\par particularly averse to having their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  push them further down\par the deflation road.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of course, the problem with this scenario is that in a race to the\par bottom, somebody's got to lose. If so many of the world's economies are\par seeking to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relatively weak, the question arises of\par which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  picks up the slack.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Up until now, the euro has mostly been the one stepping into the\par breach. With concerns about the unwieldy current account deficit in the\par U.S. and relatively low interest rates hurting the dollar, the euro has\par benefited as the clearest and most liquid alternative to the U.S.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It has gained over 20% in the past year alone, hitting an\par all-time high of $1.1932 in late May before slipping back to around\par $1.13 now.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With Japan actively trying to manage the yen's exchange rate and the\par dollar weakness route against the Chinese yuan blocked because of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's peg to the dollar, many analysts believe the euro will\par continue to bear the brunt of the dollar's adjustment and ultimately\par head higher again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But as was made clear this week, European officials aren't going to\par continue to welcome this. Having signaled for some time that he had no\par problems with the strong euro, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim\par Duisenberg admitted Thursday - after the bank held rates steady - that\par he had indeed been uncomfortable with it.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I must confess we saw with some relief that the strong movement of\par appreciation which had been evident the month before seems at least for\par the time being to have been interrupted," Duisenberg said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For euro area elected officials struggling with stagnant growth -\par particularly in Germany, the area's largest economy - Duisenberg's\par candor likely elicited sighs of relief of their own. Indeed, as if on\par cue, German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder immediately called on the ECB\par to do more to counter the strength of the euro. Some interpreted his\par words as an effort to get the ECB to take a leaf out of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's book and\par start \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to weaken the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But his more likely intent was probably to try to get the ECB to cut\par interest rates again, which would narrow the differential between\par short-term rates in the U.S. and euro area, in the process dulling the\par shine of the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and easing the deflation shadow hanging\par over Germany.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Schroeder's comments, and those of other officials in Europe and the\par U.S. recently, also probably had a more opaque aim - to encourage China\par to revalue its peg, a move that would spread the dollar's weakness more\par equally.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow has said as much twice in the past\par month, in comments that fueled an already heightened sense in the market\par that Beijing may indeed move to alter the yuan peg to the dollar at some\par point this year. U.S. Commerce Secretary Don Evans added his voice this\par week to those calling on China to move toward a flexible exchange rate\par regime. That European officials would eagerly want this too isn't\par surprising.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "To be fair to the Europeans, one thing they can say is `we're really\par bearing the brunt of this and why,"' said Ruskin. "Implicit within\par Schroeder's comments is a desire to see the euro not shoulder the full\par burden."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In recent weeks, officials at the Fed and International Monetary Fund\par have noted the imbalance between the U.S. trade deficit and surpluses in\par China and Japan. In the absence of a pickup in global demand, this\par situation could easily grow even worse.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of course, if the U.S. economy starts to show the traction that many\par economists expect during the second half of the year, the weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par shuffle won't be as important. Strong global demand is a much more\par important source of growth than a weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . And, to the extent that\par a weaker dollar is helping the U.S. see off deflation and work its way\par back to growth, it's a plus for the entire world.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But if conditions remain weak in the U.S., it's reasonable to expect\par more and more rhetoric from policymakers, as well as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from\par some and more rate cuts from others. The chorus of officials calling on\par China to blink is also bound to get much louder.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Grainne McCarthy is Assistant Managing Editor for fixed income,\par macroeconomics and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Grainne was previously Indonesia\par bureau chief and also reported for Dow Jones Newswires in Belgium).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381;\par grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030711dz7b007vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
