\par\par \b \uc2  Dollars Ends Lower as Traders Await Data, Rate Moves \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2003\par 23:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- With little in the way of fresh economic data to push it higher, the dollar ended on a down note against its major rivals Wednesday, with a strong yen sparking yet more speculation as to whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the euro was trading at $1.1339, up from $1.1310 late Tuesday. The euro was well above its two-month low of $1.1259 hit on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 117.70 yen, down from 118.14 yen late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was buying 1.3594  francs, down sharply from 1.3684 francs on Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.6362, up from $1.6336 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  barely moved against the euro following data that showed U.S. inventories at the wholesale level unexpectedly falling in May, and most strategists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in a consolidate mood awaiting Thursday's economic statistics as well as testimony by Federal Chairman Alan Greenspan before Congress next week. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds a policy meeting also Thursday, but is expected to keep interest rates steady. \par \par \uc2  "There's a little bit of abeyance waiting for some data and ahead of next week," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Morgan Stanley in New York. "If the reflation story is validated by earnings, we could break lower [in the euro] to $1.1050 and down, but if the data don't bear it out, we could return to the $1.15 range." \par \par \uc2  After two weeks of stunning dollar advances that have brought the euro to around $1.1350 from around $1.1621 the day the Federal Reserve eased interest rates on June 25, investors are waiting to see if newfound optimism about a U.S. economic recovery will be borne out in domestic economic indicators and earnings reports. \par \par \uc2  Earnings released on Tuesday from Alcoa Inc., the first member of the Dow Jones Industrial Average to report its second-quarter earnings, showed it reporting a profit decline from the first quarter, while also noting that market conditions hadn't improved. \par \par \uc2  Strategists said investors will be watching with interest initial jobless claims for the first week of June and the import price index, both due for release early Thursday and both of which are forecast to show economic improvement. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, much of the focus of the New York trading session was on the yen, which was the key mover among the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar pierced the 118-yen level early in the day before triggering some stop-loss orders around 117.90 yen and moving towards 117.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  The move sparked the inevitable speculation as to whether Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- acting via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen and halt further advances in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is shying away [from pushing the yen too high] because of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Stewart, at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  But many traders reported scant evidence of BOJ activity in the market and not much interest among investors to sell the yen, particularly with Tokyo stocks continuing to rally. \par \par \uc2  With little in the way of heavyweight data on the menu around the world, traders will look to other \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings Thursday also, with the Bank of Korea and Monetary Policy of Singapore expected to hold rates steady, while the Bank of England is also set to decide on policy. \par \par \uc2  While economists are rock solid in their view that the ECB won't ease now, they aren't as sure about the Bank of England's monetary-policy committee, which holds its first meeting under Mervyn King as governor. \par \par \uc2  Many say \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets aren't fully positioned for a possible one-quarter percentage point cut in the BOE's repurchase rate, currently at 3.75%. In the event of a cut, losses are expected to be steeper than any rally would be on word of unchanged policy. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, despite its weaker tone, the dollar continued to bully the commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so dubbed because of the dependence of their economies on commodity prices. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the Australian dollar had fallen to 65.60 U.S. cents from 66.03 cents on Tuesday, as the New Zealand dollar pared back slightly to 58.80 cents from 58.90 cents. Against the Canadian dollar, the dollar was buying $1.3743 Canadian dollars, up sharply from C$1.3646 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Though more bullish sentiment towards global growth would ultimately help the commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar's bold moves spelled nothing but trouble for them in the short term, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I would call this a consolidation period more than a trend change, [but] you don't want to catch a falling knife" said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan, describing the commodity bloc's recent moves. "We are entering a global reflation environment, and a lot of investors are unwinding the trades they had on and are reallocating." \par \par \uc2  --Neil Keane in London contributed to this story. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  07-09-03 1753ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030709dz790058x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
