\par\par \b \uc2  BC-MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  (REPEAT). \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2003\par 04:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Repeating to South Asian services) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 7 (Reuters) - The dollar was holding steady against the yen and the euro on Monday morning, but it was restricted to a tiny range as the market awaited a barrage of U.S. earnings reports. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo dealers were also watching to see how Japanese asset markets would affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average  was up 1.48 percent at 9,689.26 at 0102 GMT, after rising almost five percent last week, its best since late November. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government bond (JGB) market was calm, recovering some of the hefty losses made last week. \par \par \uc2  "If JGBs manage to stabilise, the stock market should recover and inflows from foreigners resume. That would be Japanese yen-positive," said Marshall Gittler, Deutsche Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 118.12/17 yen , up slightly from 117.97/05 yen in late U.S. on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1470/75  versus $1.1490/96. \par \par \uc2  "Since there aren't much macro data or events to watch this week, the market will keep focusing on Japanese stocks and JGBs," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still pondering whether deflationary pressure will fade if stock prices keep rising and bond prices fall worldwide," he said. \par \par \uc2  For the euro, market participants are awaiting the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting on Thursday though most do not expect a rate cut. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a further rise in the Nikkei could put pressure on the dollar against the yen, but a hefty drop in the greenback was unlikely due to fears of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official reserves, released on Monday, amounted to a record-high $545.618 billion at the end of June, up from $543.088 billion a month earlier. \par \par \uc2  Although Japan conducted 628.9 billion yen ($5.33 billion) of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market between May 29 and June 26, the rise in reserves was relatively small due to falls in the euro against the dollar and a rise in global long-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030707dz77003uy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
