\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa: Want To Discuss L-T Rate Rise With BOJ Fukui \b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2003\par 02:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The improvement in large manufacturers' business sentiment in the Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan survey shows that corporate restructuring efforts are starting to pay off for big firms, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Large firms' structural reform efforts are clearly progressing," Shiokawa told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "It's an honest reflection of that," he said, after the BOJ's latest survey, released Tuesday morning, showed the diffusion index for large manufacturers rising to minus 5 from minus 10 in the March survey. The market had expected a reading of minus 10. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa also said the recent rise in long-term interest rates may reflect an improvement in the economy, although various other factors could be at play. \par \par \uc2  Given the recent interest rate movements, however, Shiokawa said he would like to discuss bond market developments with BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui in the near future. \par \par \uc2  "I want to ask the BOJ on how it views current developments with regard to long-term rates," Shiokawa said, adding that it was still too early to make any decision on the bond market. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's Fukui has also recently expressed a desire for coordination with the finance ministry on government bond management. A discussion between the two may give rise to the possibility of increased outright government bond purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa also said his ministry will continue to watch movements by foreign funds on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, suggesting that more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be likely in the event excessive or speculative movements push the yen higher. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign funds appear to be very quiet at the moment. But I don't think they will stay that way," Shiokawa said. "We are on guard to catch any movements in case they get active again." \par \par \uc2  The ministry said Monday it sold another Y628.9 billion in June, bringing the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year to around Y7 trillion. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been concerned that an excessively strong yen could sap the nation's exports and worsen continuing price falls. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said he would like to discuss the topic of stability in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when finance ministers of the Asia-Europe Meeting (ASEM) convene in Bali, Indonesia this weekend. \par \par \uc2  "Various Asian countries appear to be interested in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability," Shiokawa said, pointing out that the topic also came up recently when he met Indonesian President Megawati Sukarnoputri, who was in Tokyo last week. \par \par \uc2  -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by John Gibson \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030701dz710001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
