\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar mixed after manufacturing report. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2003\par 19:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 30 (Reuters) - The dollar turned mixed on Monday, displaying pockets of strength amid a sluggish trading environment after a report showed manufacturing in the American heartland grew at a weaker than expected pace in June. \par \par \uc2  After last week's smaller-than-expected cut in benchmark U.S. interest rates by the Federal Reserve, the implicit message of a U.S. economy on the mend has helped fuel a dollar rise, but the market is looking for confirmation of that view. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Ernst Welteke said on Monday interest rates in the euro zone were appropriate at 2 percent and that the Fed's rate cut did not put pressure on the ECB to cut rates. \par \par \uc2  After the manufacturing data release, the euro bounced up from support at $1.1400 - close to a six-week low - to $1.1504 before trimming back to $1.1489 , a gain of 0.55 percent from Friday's New York close. \par \par \uc2  "It seems the euro above $1.1470 is a technical break higher and people are going to look for dips to buy it back," said Tim O'Sullivan, trading manager at GAIN Capital in Warren, New Jersey. \par \par \uc2  "But there has been no pullback for the short-term traders to catch and that sets a significant tone for the week if you consider that last week the tone was all about selling euro rallies. Now it appears the reverse, that is buy the dips," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro gained some strength from the day's lows after the Bank for International Settlements said the dollar faced more downside risks now than two decades ago, although higher U.S. productivity growth should help support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The fact the BIS continues to see downside risks to the dollar when it has already corrected lower is significant," said Mitul Kotecha, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  DATA WATCH \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the National Association of Purchasing Management-Chicago business barometer, a measure of manufacturing in the U.S. Midwest, rose slightly to 52.5 in June from 52.2 in May, but under analysts' expectations for a reading of 53.0. \par \par \uc2  The reading remained above 50, indicating expansion in manufacturing, but strategists cautioned this report was not likely to change expectations for the Institute for Supply Management's national manufacturing report due on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Likewise, Thursday's June jobs report will be scrutinized for any signs the key sector might show of future strength. \par \par \uc2  "Once the ISM comes out, nobody is going to remember what the Chicago PMI number was," said Steven Englander, chief North American exchange strategist at Barclays Capital in New York. \par \par \uc2  The ISM report is expected to show manufacturing activity expanded in June for the first time after three months of contraction with a reading of 51 while the unemployment rate for June is expected to rise 0.1 percentage point to 6.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped from near session highs against the yen, trading down to 120.05 yen , up 0.38 percent, but below the two-month high hit earlier in the session of 120.23 yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength in June totaled 628.9 billion yen, a sharp decline from some four trillion yen in May, its largest ever monthly volume of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains against the Swiss franc were cut back as well, trading to 1.3535 francs , a gain of 0.37 percent on the day after trading as high as 1.3612 francs. \par \par \uc2  The euro surged to a near three year high against the Swiss franc of 1.5560 francs , before drifting back to 1.5532 francs, a gain of 0.75 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  Sterling turned around and headed higher against the greenback as well, trading at $1.6528 , up 0.22 percent, after hitting a low of $1.6457. \par \par \uc2  The greenback's gain against the Canadian dollar eroded to C$1.3533 , up 0.40 percent on the day, down from a session high of C$1.3620. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030630dz6u00z1a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
