\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar blips up vs yen as Fed fails to excite. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2003\par 04:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 26 (Reuters) - The dollar drifted higher against the yen on Thursday helped by worries about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Federal Reserve's rate cut failed to give the market clear direction. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks and Treasuries reacted negatively to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cut of 25 basis points to a four-decade low of one percent, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was unfazed. \par \par \uc2  By 0200 GMT, the dollar was at 118.06 yen after firming to the day's high of 118.31. It was at 117.93 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The euro was flat at $1.1531 Against the yen, it was also steady at 136.13 yen versus 136.11 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The market is watching whether the Fed will cut rates in the future. But in the near term, with the Fed's decision out of the way, dollar/yen is seen supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caution," said Shogo Nagaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the dollar spiked up from around 117.40 yen to above 118 yen on speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I get the feeling that the Japanese authorities are uncomfortable when the dollar is under 118 yen," said a trader at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Finance Ministry officials declined to comment on whether the government had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  MIXED SENTIMENT \par \par \uc2  Sentiment for the greenback was mixed with the market reluctant to bid the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too strongly as the Fed left open the option of cutting interest rates further if inflation dips more than expected, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Fed reduced its funds rate to a low last seen in 1958, but the market had been leaning toward a half-point reduction. \par \par \uc2  The Fed, in a statement following the cut, said it saw firmer spending, improved financial conditions and signs of stabilising labour markets. But it also noted the economy had yet to show sustainable growth. \par \par \uc2  "I think that the Fed's view was pretty bullish," said Koichi Abe, manager at Aozora Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading section. \par \par \uc2  "If the stock market heads up again, it could prove that they were right and might lead to dollar-buying." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market is now focusing on whether the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) will ease further following its 50 basis point cut in the benchmark interest rate to two percent earlier in the month. \par \par \uc2  Ernst Welteke, a member of the ECB governing council and president of the Bundesbank, said inflation in Europe would have to fall even further over the medium term before interest rates in the euro zone could come down any further. \par \par \uc2  He made the comment in an interview released on Wednesday by German daily Boersen-Zeitung. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030626dz6q00brz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
