\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Closes Mixed, as Greenback Gains on Euro \b0\par\par\uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2003\par 00:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Buoyed by a greater conviction among traders that the Federal Reserve\par will only cut interest rates modestly this week, the dollar enjoyed a\par burst of life against the euro Monday, climbing at one point to its\par strongest level in over a month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  But in a sign that the dollar may not have embarked on a more sustained turnaround, it lost ground against its other key rivals. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the jump in the dollar against the euro -- which was also weaker against the British pound and the Japanese yen -- had more to do with a clearing out of long-standing positions than anything more fundamental. \par \par \uc2  "It's partly a dollar move [today]," said Bob Lynch, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. "But we've also seen a retracing, an unwinding of a sizable euro rally. ... This is follow-through euro selling." \par \par \uc2  In late trading on Monday, the euro was at $1.1539, down from $1.1604 late Friday. The dollar was at 117.59 yen, down from 118.29 yen late Friday in New York. Sterling was changing hands at $1.6674, compared with $1.6618 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Firming expectations that the Fed might only deliver a quarter-percentage-point cut in the federal funds rate after its two-day meeting have given market participants something to hang their hats on. A more-modest easing somewhat reduces the risk of investors shifting funds out of the U.S. towards higher-yielding assets, something that has weighed on the dollar for months. \par \par \uc2  "It's a combination of people realizing the Fed interest rate cut will not be as aggressive as people first thought -- that the interest gap with the ECB that narrowed will not be widened by the Fed -- and also that U.S. economic fundamentals seem to be definitely improving," said Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with MG Financial in New York. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, some analysts predict continued weak conditions in Europe and stronger than expected data in the U.S. could trigger a longer-term firming of the dollar. That sense was partly behind the euro's dip as low as $1.1508, its weakest point since May 16. \par \par \uc2  "We've been suggesting for weeks that the dollar was heading for a cyclical rally," said Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with Citibank in New York. "This is about as solid a set of growth-supportive conditions as we've had in the U.S. for the last 20 years." \par \par \uc2  The market also took cues from a languishing European economy and what some deem the increasing likelihood of another European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut. If the ECB cuts rates at its July 10 meeting, as some analysts forecast, the euro will likely weaken further as investment in Europe becomes less attractive. \par \par \uc2  "We've gone through two weeks of lack of volatility in the dollar, but despite comments from ECB officials, investors have to prepare for an eventual ECB rate cut," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains on the euro came despite a selloff in U.S. stocks and rally in the government bond market. The Dow Jones Industrial Average ended the day down 1.4%, while the Nasdaq Composite Index lost 2%. \par \par \uc2  The dollar softened against its other principal rival, the yen, in a move that strategists attributed to the pressure of the pullback of the euro against the yen. The risk of a further strengthening of the yen is tempered by possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- via the Bank of Japan -- around 117.50 yen, said analysts. \par \par \uc2  Having allegedly spent around $34 billion during the month of May \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen, the BOJ hasn't been as much of a visible force in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this month. But analysts believe it has been conducting some covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's desire to stop a strong yen from derailing a very fragile recovery, stands ready to do so again. \par \par \uc2  Write to Simona Covel at simona.covel@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Michael Deibert contributed to this article) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  06-23-03 1818ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030623dz6n005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
