\par\par \b \uc2  Treasurys Fall, Dollar Leaps As Mkts Reassess Fed Calls \b0\par\par\uc2  By Agnes T. Crane and Michael Mackenzie \par \uc2 1,060 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2003\par 20:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Having bet almost unanimously that the Federal Reserve will cut interest rates aggressively next week, bond market investors are no longer so cock sure, a change of heart that's causing a swift drop in prices. \par \par \uc2  The reversal in sentiment follows some stronger than expected economic data that helped brighten an otherwise gloomy outlook on the U.S. economy and diminished concerns that deflation might be lurking on the horizon. The changed market view is sparking jitters about whether, in the wake of a remarkably profitable period for bond investors, the rally is about to come to the kind of resounding halt that would send investors scurrying into other asset markets. \par \par \uc2  "The current market is showing many of the signs that suggest this bond market rally could end badly if both economic growth and inflation tick higher," said Colin Lundgren, senior portfolio manager for fixed-income at American Express Asset Management Group in Minneapolis, which has over $7 billion U.S. fixed income assets under management. \par \par \uc2  In many ways of course, it's far too early to call an end to the bond market party. Although yields have backed up this week, they remain historically low and have rallied sharply since the May 6 Federal Open Market Committee statement first flagged disinflation as a possible concern. In comments since then, Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan further focused the bond market's attention on price pressures, by clearly stating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to take out insurance against what he called "corrosive deflation." In the opaque language of Fed officials, that was tantamount to saying "we will cut rates." \par \par \uc2  But a government report Tuesday, showing inflation jumped much more than expected, pulled the rug out from under the bond market's solid conviction on the economy. The consumer price index's core rate, excluding food and energy, rose 0.3%, clocking up its biggest gain in 10 months and beating expectations of a 0.1% increase. \par \par \uc2  This hasn't been sufficient to push large flows out of Treasurys into stocks, but it has certainly sparked a correction in bond prices that could gain more traction if economic data and price pressures continue to surprise to the upside. For now, the dollar is rallying and Treasury bulls are retreating. \par \par \uc2  "No one probably thinks the Fed is going to do 50 anymore," said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Bear in New York. "The shift in attitude over the course of the week is making the dollar better bid." \par \par \uc2  The two-year Treasury note, used by market participants to gauge future Fed policy action, has backed off the record low of 1.056% touched in intra-day trade last Friday and was trading around 1.24% Wednesday afternoon, only one basis point below the current federal funds target of 1.25%. The 10-year note was yielding 3.35%, up from its close Friday of 3.12%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Sticking To Rate Cut Calls\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To be sure, the government bond market isn't backing away from a Fed\par easing altogether. For one, Fed officials haven't said anything to\par convince them to take a cut off the table. In that context, the shakeout\par in the bond market appears more like a correction than a fundamental\par reassessment of market skepticism about a second half recovery in the\par U.S. economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The FOMC will likely cut the funds rate by 25 basis points to 1% as\par "they have built up expectations that they will do something," said Jay\par Mueller, economist at Strong capital Management in Milwaukee Wis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Given that the massive decline in long term yields to fresh 45-year\par lows over the past six weeks had been driven by expectations of falling\par inflation and the possible emergence of deflation, the uptick in core\par inflation has caused some repositioning in Treasurys, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics in\par Valhalla, New York, said the FOMC's "decision will come to the wire." He\par reckons more soggy economic data will embolden the Fed to take more\par aggressive action.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's because the week isn't over yet and the market still faces two\par important pieces of economic data that could cause yet another\par reassessment by investors. Both initial jobless claims and the\par Philadelphia Federal Reserve Bank survey on regional manufacturing\par figure large on the radar since they have the potential to alter or\par affirm existing sentiment toward the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While economists expect a small decline of 5,000 in the number of\par first-time jobless claims filed in the week ended June 14, it will\par remain above 400,000, suggesting that the labor market remains weak. A\par better than expected read on claims is likely to deepen speculation that\par the Fed will ease only 25 basis points, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, manufacturing activity in the Philadelphia area is expected\par to have picked up in June, with Wall Street economists forecasting the\par Philadelphia Fed index at 5, from -4.8 in the previous month. A stronger\par read on the region's manufacturing activity would follow a better than\par expected New York Fed manufacturing survey earlier this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Could Still Go Either Way\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In any case, it's not just Fed uncertainty that is hurting Treasurys.\par Improved sentiment for agency debt - severely pressured last week in the\par wake of Freddie Mac (FRE) firing key management figures amid an ongoing\par restatement of earnings back to 2000 which subsequently prompted a\par criminal probe - has stemmed flows  out of agencies into Treasurys.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Then there's the better performance of the much beleaguered dollar. It\par has firmed against chief rivals to such an extent that market\par participants say Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has worked hard to\par depress its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year, has stepped back and will let the\par market do its work for it.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bond market participants believe that respite by Japanese monetary\par authorities was weighing on Treasurys Wednesday as less dollar buying by\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  means less investment in Treasurys, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's preferred\par repository for the dollars it buys when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     -Agnes T. Crane and Michael Mackenzie; Dow Jones Newswires;\par 201-938-2122; 201-938-5451; email agnes.crane@dowjones.com;\par michael.mackenzie@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030618dz6i006sh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
