\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China yuan fwd premium pulls back from record high. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2003\par 05:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 17 (Reuters) - Forward premiums on the Chinese yuan pulled back from record highs hit early on Tuesday after China said it would maintain its policy of keeping the exchange rate stable. \par \par \uc2  The one-year non-deliverable forward (NDF) dollar was dealt at a discount of 1,900 points in early trade after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said he had heard China was interested in moving to a more flexible exchange rate system and said the United States would back any plan by China to do so. \par \par \uc2  But officials from China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters there was no change to the policy of keeping the yuan , also known as the renminbi, in a narrow band of 8.2760 to 8.2800 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  That knocked the one-year NDF back down to 1,650 points, implying a yuan worth about 8.11 per dollar in 12 months time, a rise of about two percent. \par \par \uc2  Trading in the NDFs was volatile. Bankers said volumes were thin, and deals were for about five to 10 million dollars each. \par \par \uc2  The yuan premium hit a then-record 1,500 points on Monday, breaching January's high, before getting another boost from Snow. \par \par \uc2  "China has indicated, I gather, to other leaders that they intend to create more flexibility on the renminbi and they are to be encouraged in doing so," Snow said on Monday, but Chinese authorities held a different view. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to maintain our stable exchange rate regime," a State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters. "This is our basic policy for now." \par \par \uc2  Forwards on the Hong Kong dollar , which is pegged at 7.8 per U.S. dollar, have risen from 140 to 190 points in four days. Markets believe the yuan and the HK dollar will eventually converge and so trade the HK \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a discount to the U.S. dollar in the forward market. \par \par \uc2  REVALUATION \par \par \uc2  NDFs on the yuan have surged on speculation China would widen its trading band, which has been criticised as undervaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and making China's exports too competitive. \par \par \uc2  The revaluation trades were boosted by a Goldman Sachs report last week suggesting China would allow the yuan to appreciate by 2.5 percent in six months, and five percent in one year. \par \par \uc2  The perception that China had weathered the Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) outbreak with no major adverse impact on growth has also been driving yuan premiums up. \par \par \uc2  U.S. trade data released on Friday showed the United States had a $34 billion trade deficit with China in January-April, 28.6 percent wider than in the first four months of 2002, and focusing more attention on whether the yuan was undervalued. \par \par \uc2  SCEPTICISM \par \par \uc2  Making money out of the volatility in yuan forwards during this speculative frenzy was one thing, analysts said, but betting China would actually revalue the yuan was complicated. \par \par \uc2  Lehman Brothers argued in a report China would wait until the second half of 2004 before making any changes, with policymakers likely to focus on domestic growth and structural reform. \par \par \uc2  Barclays Capital said in their report a revaluation over the next 12 months could not be ruled out, but they thought it was unlikely and at the most would be very modest. \par \par \uc2  "Trade frictions caused by the undervalued yuan are not of sufficient scale to threaten a yuan revaluation at present, especially with the main critic of the yuan peg - Japan - reducing the force of its argument following the $34 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May," it said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030617dz6h0060u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
