\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends losses vs euro, eyes on BoJ. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2003\par 10:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 16 (Reuters) - The dollar extended its latest losses against the euro on Monday as rising expectations that the Federal Reserve would cut interest rates aggressively depressed it to within half a cent of record lows. Speculation swirled in the market during Asian trade that the dollar's latest fall had prompted Japanese authorities to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again to cap the yen's rise against the struggling greenback. \par \par \uc2  Wariness of the Bank of Japan briefly helped the dollar, which has been under pressure since late Friday after unexpectedly weak U.S. consumer sentiment data. \par \par \uc2  "One issue today is whether Japan was actually in the market," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like Japan is acting again on its new policy of keeping dollar/yen in range trade. The other thing the market is focusing on, apart from new U.S. data, is U.S. stocks." \par \par \uc2  By 0750 GMT, the dollar was down a quarter percent from late Friday at $1.1911 per euro and up 0.10 percent on the yen at 117.52. \par \par \uc2  BOJ FEARED \par \par \uc2  BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation was prompted by a series of aggressive bids from a few major U.S. operators when the dollar threatened to fall below 117 yen on Monday, a level not touched in the past few weeks. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers were unsure whether the BOJ was behind the U.S. operators' bids that emerged around noon in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  constantly underpins dollar/yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold yen for dollars in heavy doses a number of times this year to protect its exports - seen as essential for the nation's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "I don't know if the authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  today. But it's clear they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar falls," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi offered a fresh verbal warning against the yen's rise on Monday, saying the yen was in no position to strengthen in the longer term. \par \par \uc2  U.S. RATE DECISION LOOMS \par \par \uc2  But markets were also focusing on the upcoming rate decision by the U.S. Federal Reserve after a surprisingly weak reading in Friday's U.S. consumer data drove up speculation that the Fed could move aggressively to boost the weak economy. \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan said its gauge of consumer sentiment slipped to 87.2 in June from May's 92.1, contrary to economists' predictions that the figure would rise. \par \par \uc2  The data fuelled speculation the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut by 50 basis points rather than 25 basis points when it holds its policy meeting on June 24 and 25. \par \par \uc2  A bigger rate cut is thought to be negative for the dollar, as it would widen the yield gap between the greenback and the euro. \par \par \uc2  Data releases on Monday include euro zone industrial output at 1000 GMT and the New York Fed's manufacturing survey at 1200. \par \par \uc2  The market was also eyeing Tuesday's consumer price index (CPI) for May amid growing concerns in the United States about deflation. The CPI was expected to register no change in May after a 0.3 percent decline in April. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg was due to speak at an evening event in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  Swiss National Bank Chairman Jean-Pierre Roth speaks at 1800 GMT in Warsaw. On Friday, the Swiss franc plunged against the euro after Roth signalled the SNB would not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030616dz6g00goj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
