\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar crawls up - BOJ string-pulling suspected. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2003\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 16 (Reuters) - The dollar rose against the yen on Monday as aggressive bids from a few major U.S. operators prompted speculation that the Bank of Japan may have stepped in to weaken the Japanese unit. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said the Japanese authorities may have bought the dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened to fall below 117 yen, a level not touched in the past few weeks. \par \par \uc2  Others were unsure whether the BOJ was behind the U.S. operators' bids that emerged around noon in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Still, wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold yen for dollars in heavy doses a number of times this year to protect its exports - seen as essential for the nation's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "I don't know if the authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  today. But it's clear they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar falls," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Traders suggested that a surprisingly weak reading in Friday's U.S. consumer confidence data caused the Japanese authorities to fret that the dollar's recent slide - from a high of 119.66 yen two weeks ago - would gain momentum. \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan said that its gauge of consumer sentiment slipped to 87.2 in June from the previous month's 92.1, contrary to economists' predictions that the figure would rise. \par \par \uc2  The data fueled speculation that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut by 50 basis points rather than 25 basis points when it holds its policy meeting on June 24 and 25. \par \par \uc2  A bigger rate cut is thought to be negative for the dollar, as it would widen the interest rate gap between the greenback and the euro. \par \par \uc2  "For now dealers are keen to sell the dollar as they anxiously wait for the FOMC (Federal Open Market Committee) meeting following Friday's weak U.S. data," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinsei Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is expected to be more vulnerable to speculative sales as the market braces for results," he said. \par \par \uc2  DEFLATION TALK \par \par \uc2  The market is also eyeing Tuesday's consumer price index (CPI) for May for a clue to determine the size of a possible rate cut by the Fed and amid growing concerns in the United States about deflation. \par \par \uc2  The CPI is expected to register no change in May after a 0.3 percent decline in April. \par \par \uc2  At 0545 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 117.62/67 yen up from the day's low of 117.16 and against Friday's late New York level of 117.39/47. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1872/77 little changed from Friday's $1.1868/74. Many traders think the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could test a record high of $1.1935 - set in late May - this week. \par \par \uc2  It was at 139.64/76 yen up from the late New York level of 139.39/50. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi offered a fresh verbal warning against the yen's rise on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi said that the yen was in no position to strengthen in the longer term. He also said the U.S. economy remains strong relative to Europe and Japan. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030616dz6g00dfr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
