\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar on defensive, market braces for Fed rate cut. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2003\par 05:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 16 (Reuters) - The dollar was on the defensive against the yen and euro on Monday as speculation the United States would ease interest rates heated up following weaker-than-expected consumer sentiment data, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Focus is shifting back to U.S. fundamentals, with the market keenly speculating on the amount of a possible rate reduction at the Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee (FOMC) meeting due to begin on June 24, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Results of the two-day meeting are expected on June 25. \par \par \uc2  "For now dealers are keen to sell the dollar as they anxiously wait for the FOMC meeting following Friday's weak U.S. data," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinsei Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is expected to be more vulnerable to speculative sales as the market braces for results," he said. \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan said on Friday that its gauge of consumer sentiment slipped to 87.2 in June from the previous month's 92.1, contrary to economists' predictions that the figure would rise. \par \par \uc2  The market is eyeing Tuesday's consumer price index (CPI) for May for a clue to determine the size of a possible rate cut by the Fed. \par \par \uc2  The CPI, which gauges inflation, is expected to register no change in May after a 0.3 percent decline in April. \par \par \uc2  At 0300 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 117.31/36 yen against Friday's late New York level of 117.39/47. \par \par \uc2  The euro regained strength, with a record high above $1.1935 in the market's scope again. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at $1.1883/88 against Friday's $1.1868/74. It was at 139.36/44 yen hardly changed from the late New York level of 139.39/50. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Ernst Welteke said the recent dollar depreciation may help the United States control its current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  Because investors have put less money to work in capital markets as the global economy has struggled and U.S. economic imbalances have widened, the dollar has fallen prey to the downdraft of the trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR SUPPORT \par \par \uc2  The greenback found support against the yen as the market remained nervous about selling it strongly following a fresh verbal warning from Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi said that the yen was in no position to strengthen in the longer term. He also said the U.S. economy remains strong relative to Europe and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is thought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yen's appreciation since early June. Most recently the Japanese authorities were suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  discretely on Friday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "They could be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  constantly, while the market is increasingly becoming reluctant to trade in dollar/yen," said a trader at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030616dz6g00796\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
