\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro mired in range trade, Swissie dominates. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2003\par 14:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 13 (Reuters) - The euro moved in tight ranges against the dollar on Friday ahead of U.S. economic data, but held firmly to gains on the Swiss franc which was knocked down by bearish comments from the Swiss National Bank. \par \par \uc2  The economic data, including trade figures and a consumer sentiment survey, is expected to sustain market views that the Federal Reserve will cut interest rates soon. \par \par \uc2  With little change seen in market sentiment towards the dollar, investors were focused on Switzerland's economic woes and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledges to prevent the franc from rising. \par \par \uc2  "The market is waiting for U.S. data, and the Swissie is the only area where things are happening," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at WestLB in London. \par \par \uc2  The Swissie matched recent near three-year lows on the euro at 1.5498 after SNB chairman Jean-Pierre Roth signalled the bank would not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to cap the franc's strength and protect Switzerland's battered economy. \par \par \uc2  The euro initially rallied against the dollar as well on the back of his comments, which sent investors scrambling to buy euros for francs. \par \par \uc2  But the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned back south after hitting session highs at $1.1801, when it failed to make a sustained break above the closely-watched $1.18 level and drifted back down to $1.1750 by 1150 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The euro failed to break the $1.18 sustainably already yesterday and there was quite heavy selling at that level again," Klawitter said. \par \par \uc2  BITTER SWISS PROSPECTS \par \par \uc2  Switzerland's economic woes grabbed the spotlight with SNB's Roth repeatedly signalling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not be ruled out with no more room left to cut the SNB's near-zero interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The SNB fears a strong franc would hurt the export-dependent Switzerland's chances for recovery. \par \par \uc2  It already abandoned its March forecast the economy could grow nearly one percent in 2003, predicting stagnation as dole queues grow longer amid a slump in demand from key markets. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the safe-haven franc stands to gain if the dollar continues to fall and risk aversion plagues markets. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the franc shed more than half a percent since the previous session's close at 1.3188. \par \par \uc2  "Roth's comments are consistent with what he's said previously, that the SNB will counter any strength in the franc," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank Securities in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's clear the one major policy available to the SNB to curb any significant franc strength will be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  FOCUS ON RATES \par \par \uc2  The U.S. University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey is due at 1345 GMT with the index forecast to rise for the third straight month, although the pace of improvement would be slower than in April and May. \par \par \uc2  The index is forecast to rise to 93.4 from 92.1 in May, led by a pick-up in current conditions rather than expectations. \par \par \uc2  "There is this expectation that the Fed is going to cut rates by 50 basis points no matter what the data shows, which is keeping the dollar on the weak side," said Neil Parker, market strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  Trading in the yen was subdued with investors wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady on the day on the dollar at 117.69 and up on the euro at 138.25. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers suspected the Japanese authorities had stepped in on Thursday when the dollar fell towards 117.40 yen after a mixed bag of U.S. indicators. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030613dz6d00nmg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
