\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Bad News Is Good For Stocks \b0\par\par\uc2 2,240 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2003\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Investors Are In A Buying Mood - Period\par DJIA          9183.22    gain 128.33    up 1.4%\par NASDAQ        1646.02    gain 18.35     up 1.1%\par S&P 500       997.48     gain 12.64     up 1.3%\par Dow Future    9186.00    gain 9.00      up 0.1%\par NASDAQ Future 1230.00    gain 1.50      up 0.1%\par S&P Future    998.00     gain 0.75      up 0.1%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.20% up 0.02\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)-  Wall Street extended its rally Wednesday as investors shook off a warning from Texas Instruments and sent stocks higher on optimism about the economy. Profit-taking hit Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: The Dow Jones industrials climbed above the 9,100 mark for the first time in 11 months. \par \par \uc2  "The market momentum here is quite strong," said Steven Goldman, chief market strategist, Weeden & Co. in Greenwich, Conn. "When we have a sustainable advance, market horizons tend to expand, with investors willing to look further into the future and shake off bad news." \par \par \uc2  Texas Instruments fell $1.53 to $18.86 after the chip maker lowered its second-quarter outlook, citing in part sluggish sales due to SARS fears in Asia. \par \par \uc2  "What's interesting in my opinion is that we've seen pieces of bad news that the markets could have responded to negatively, but unlike the last couple of years, the market isn't responding," said Subodh Kumar, chief investment strategist for CIBC World Markets. \par \par \uc2  "When a company issued a warning or there was an accounting scandal it used to tar the market, but now investors are just ascribing it to the individual company and not to the market as a whole," he said. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: A wave of profit-taking in Treasurys amid sharply higher stocks drove prices of government securities across the board moderately lower Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Longer maturities, which rallied sharply and made historic lows in yields in the morning, fell back into negative territory along with shorter maturities. \par \par \uc2  Early demand for Treasurys was fueled by mortgage hedging and continued selling of agency debt after the U.S. Attorney's Office in Alexandria, Va., confirmed Wednesday that it has opened a criminal investigation related to Monday's shakeup and accounting trouble at Freddie Mac. \par \par \uc2  Freddie Mac responded to the news of the investigation by stating that "we will continue to cooperate in all respects" and added that the conduct disclosed wasn't related to accounting records but related to former President and Chief Operating Officer David Glenn's diaries. \par \par \uc2  Amid the morning rally, the benchmark 10-year note yield hit a 45-year low of 3.15% and the yield on long bonds set a new all-time low at 4.20%. \par \par \uc2  But the rally fizzled quickly midday after Freddie Mac said it plans to buy back a total of $10 billion of its own debt Thursday and Friday. \par \par \uc2  David Coard, head of fixed-income trading at Williams Capital Group in New York, said he wasn't surprised that investors took profits, "when you reached record low yields." \par \par \uc2  In economic news Wednesday, the Federal Reserve's Beige Book showed the U.S. economy saw some signs of recovery but mostly stayed sluggish during April and May. \par \par \uc2  There was little reaction to the report from the Treasury market. And rate cut odds, as shown in fed fund futures, remained at 40% for a 0.5 point Fed cut at the June 24-25 policy meeting following the Beige Book, "which contained little new information about the pace of economic growth," said Miller Tabak's chief bond market strategist Tony Crescenzi. \par \par \uc2  Participants say data Thursday - the May retail sales report - will be crucial to the financial market, as the report sheds light on how the state of the economy after the Iraq War. \par \par \uc2  Everything is in place for the economy to resume growing strongly, Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas President Robert McTeer said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  McTeer said: "Everything is lined up just about right for growth to resume at a more vigorous pace - but it just hasn't happened yet." \par \par \uc2  The one weak link, McTeer said, was business investment. "Businesses are shell-shocked; they are waiting to see profits before they are willing to invest more," McTeer said. "And their very waiting for that is causing it not to happen rapidly enough. \par \par \uc2  "But it will happen," he said. "We always do get out of these things." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japan Equities For Wall Street Higher\par USD-Yen             117.70       loss  0.04    dn 0.03%\par AUD-USD             0.6636       gain  0.0005  up 0.06%\par Nikkei 225          8962.83      gain  72.07   up 0.8%\par Hang Seng           9713.87      gain  51.81   up 0.5%\par JGB Yield           0.4450% up 0.0050\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks were higher Thursday for the\par second-straight day, following resilient advances on Wall Street.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But traders said sellers loomed at levels around 9000 in the benchmark\par Nikkei.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bargain-hunters kept losses in Japanese government bond prices to a\par minimum.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's industrial output fell 1.5% in April, the government said\par Thursday, revising production data to show a steeper decline than it\par initially reported two weeks ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Share Prices On A Roll - But For How Long?\par Euro-USD            1.1764      gain  0.0009  up 0.08%\par Stlg-USD            1.6694      gain  0.0026  up 0.2%\par USD-Franc           1.3070      loss  0.0015  dn 0.1%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European share prices should start higher, with\par prices of government bonds and the euro expected little changed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares should start the session mildly higher,\par although major indices are approaching levels that may give even bullish\par investors pause.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks rose for a second consecutive session Wednesday as\par heavyweight financials led bourses higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, technology stocks underperformed the market, pushed lower by\par retreating chip stocks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said the key question for investors is how long the current\par market momentum can last.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The U.S. is clearly leading the way," said Anais Faraj, strategist at\par Nomura Securities. "If U.S. indexes consolidate by maintaining their\par post-March gains over the summer, we would look to raise our exposure to\par equities," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Although key economic indicators have improved, any hint of\par disappointment in Q3 earnings could push markets firmly back into the\par bear trend. Equally, a relapse in the jobs market could undermine belief\par in the consumer-led recovery," Faraj added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Simon Rubinsohn, economist at Gerard, said the prospects for U.S.\par growth and earnings remain key elements in any durable market recovery.\par "In order for equities to maintain upward momentum, investors will need\par confirmation that the U.S. economy is continuing to experience post-war\par rejuvenation," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Takeover speculation helped lift shares in Allianz, Europe's largest\par insurer, for a second consecutive session. Shares rose 2.9% to EUR74.90\par on continued speculation that it will offload its stake in Dresdner Bank\par to Bank of America. Commerzbank, also tipped as a takeover target,\par gained 5.5% to EUR12.46.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices should open little changed\par ahead of more comments from European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  leaders and the ECB's\par monthly report.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bonds were largely unchanged Wednesday, though\par the shortest dates suffered slightly from rate-cut uncertainty.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late Tuesday and Wednesday morning the market was given something of a\par roller-coaster ride by rate speculation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par First, newspaper reports citing ECB President Wim Duisenberg that\par further rate cuts are possible triggered a rally.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, in a TV interview Wednesday morning, Duisenberg backtracked\par somewhat on his comments to newspapers, pushing bond prices lower.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Most economists still expect the ECB to cut interest rates further\par this year, although probably not in the immediate future. Bond prices\par recovered in line with these expectations.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session at around $1.1750, but needs to\par break out higher soon or face a longer period of consolidation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar softened against its transatlantic counterparts Wednesday\par on continued expectations of a U.S. interest rate cut, while fears of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept the greenback steady against the\par yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Recent comments by Federal Reserve officials have fueled speculation\par of a rate cut at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's June 24-25 policy meeting.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Most comments that have come out from Fed officials over the last\par couple of days have been relatively dovish, in the sense that they have\par been consistent with the prevailing market view that the Fed will cut\par rates at the late June meeting," said Nick Bennenbroek, senior economist\par at Brown Brothers Harriman & Co. in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bennenbroek said the market is in "wait-and-see mode," and will be\par watching Thursday's retail sales data for May. On Friday, the University\par of Michigan will release its preliminary results for consumer sentiment\par in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Andrew Chaveriat, technical analyst at BNP Paribas, said the euro,\par pound and other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are taking a breather before resuming their\par strengthening trend against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro strengthened during the London session after European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg and Deutsche Bundesbank President Ernst\par Welteke both commented that it was too early to discuss further rate\par cuts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Thursday, June 12: ECB, US Retail Sales\par ET/GMT\par 0030/0430 Japan        April Revised Industrial Production\par 0100/0500 Japan        June Bank Of Japan Report\par 0200/0600 Germany      April Current Account (seen EUR9.8 Bln)\par 0200/0600 Germany      April Trade Balance (seen EUR4.8 Bln)\par 0245/0645 France       Apr Industrial Production (seen -0.2%)\par 0300/0700 Italy        Final May Consumer Price Index\par 0330/0730 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Duisenberg\par 0400/0800 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monthly Bulletin\pa