\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Falls Against European Peers, Steady Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 784 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2003\par 00:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar softened against its transatlantic counterparts Wednesday\par on continued expectations of a U.S. interest-rate cut, while fears of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  steady against\par the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the euro was trading at $1.1751, up from $1.1677 late Tuesday. The dollar was trading at 117.77 yen, little changed from 117.68 yen late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.6670, up from $1.6509 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  After losing ground in the Asian and European sessions, the dollar had stalled its descent by the start of New York trading and remained in narrow ranges through the rest of the day as market participants awaited further guidance, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Recent comments by Federal Reserve officials have fueled speculation of a rate cut at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy meeting in two weeks, with the market looking for additional indications Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Most comments that have come out from Fed officials over the last couple of days have been relatively dovish in the sense that they have been consistent with the prevailing market view that the Fed will cut rates at the late June meeting," said Nick Bennenbroek, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman & Co. in New York. \par \par \uc2  He added the market is in "wait-and-see mode" and will be watching Thursday's retail sales data for May. On Friday, the University of Michigan will release its preliminary results for consumer sentiment in June. \par \par \uc2  Andrew Chaveriat, technical analyst at BNP Paribas, said the euro, pound and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are taking a breather before resuming their strengthening trend against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened during the London session after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg and Deutsche Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke both commented it was too early to discuss further rate cuts. In contrast to earlier newspaper reports, Mr. Duisenberg also said in the television interview he didn't know whether the ECB has more room to maneuver on interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar keeps testing downside resistance at 117.50 yen. If it breaks through 117.50 yen or 117.30 yen, it could fall to 117 yen, said Mr. Chaveriat. \par \par \uc2  But he noted that while the dollar is "still a bit heavy," investors are wary that the Bank of Japan "may be lurking down there." Japanese monetary authorities have a long history of direct or covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's appreciation against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Vice Chairman Roger Ferguson gave a mixed assessment of the U.S. economy, but didn't discourage expectations of lower rates. He called recent statistics on production and employment "disappointing" and cited the "considerable uncertainty" of the near-term outlook for the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, he noted reduced risks related to the war in Iraq and said recent corporate-earnings reports "have caused the tenor of the financial market to improve noticeably." \par \par \uc2  Mr. Ferguson said later the Fed doesn't want to see "inflation by most measures slip much lower." \par \par \uc2  The Fed's Beige Book report for April and May supported his view on the economy, showing some signs of recovery but remaining mostly sluggish. Six of the Fed's 12 districts said conditions were mixed or improving in economic activity, while the other six generally said growth was "subdued, sluggish or subpar." \par \par \uc2  Other Fed officials to take to the podium Wednesday generally stayed away from discussing monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Gov. Susan Bies said accounting problems brought to light this week at Freddie Mac haven't had an immediate impact on the U.S. banking system. She added the Fed is monitoring investigations by other federal authorities but isn't conducting its own probe. \par \par \uc2  Also Wednesday, Freddie Mac disclosed the Securities and Exchange Commission has opened a formal inquiry into the company. The U.S. Attorney's Office in Alexandria, Va., confirmed  it has opened a criminal investigation into the management shakeup and accounting trouble at government-sponsored mortgage-finance firm. \par \par \uc2  However, the latest headlines about Freddie Mac had little effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, which remained steady during the New York session. Troubles at the mortgage-finance giant could have serious consequences for the dollar if foreign investors begin to sell off their massive holdings of agency debt, but so far there have been no signs of any such flight. \par \par \uc2  Write to Tom Barkley at tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Robert Flint and Simona Covel in New York and Jennifer Corbett Dooren in Washington contributed to this story) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  06-11-03 1820ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030611dz6b006y1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
