\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Split Takes On Japan Econ Fuel Stock, JGB Rallies \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2 895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2003\par 12:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Contradictory takes on Japan's economic outlook fueled stock and bond rallies Wednesday, with shares rising on guarded optimism among foreign investors but debt prices gaining on domestic investors' continued pessimism. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei 225 Stock Average rose to a fresh six-month high, briefly topping 9000 for the first time since December 5 in heavy trading. The Nikkei ended the session up 1.2% at 8890.30, while the broader Topix index rose 0.6% to 874.70. \par \par \uc2  Stock trading volume, at 1.771 billion shares, was at the highest level since 1989 and was above the one-billion mark for the 10th straight session. \par \par \uc2  The surge in share prices left the Nikkei well off its 20-year low of 7603.76 on April 28. The Topix index, at 874.70, is 14% above its 770.46 nadir hit on April 14. \par \par \uc2  "Fears among institutional investors - both local and overseas - about the risk of not holding Tokyo stocks in portfolios are growing sharply," said a strategist at a local brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japanese government bonds continued to defy gravity, with super-long cash bonds leading the market again as 10-, 20- and 30-year bond yields fell to new record lows. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year JGB yield dropped 3.5 basis points to a new low of 0.43%, just two days after it fell below 0.5% for the first time ever. The 20-year yield marked a record low of 0.745%, while the 30-year yield fell to an all-time low of 0.96%. \par \par \uc2  The rallies in the two markets build on steady gains for both stocks and bonds in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  Foreigners have been leading the share market's recovery as gains in the U.S. and other bourses give them extra cash to spend and make Japanese shares look cheap by comparison. Their buying of Japanese equities has pushed up the yen, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in records amounts to prevent a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from crushing the fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, domestic investors - the biggest players by far in the JGB market - continue to believe Japan's economic outlook remains grim and deflation is here to stay, and so are pouring their money into bonds. \par \par \uc2  It's unusual for stocks to rise sharply while bond yields fall to historical lows. Stock prices tend to rise when investors become more upbeat about corporate profits and a stronger economy, while rock-bottom bond yields are associated with entrenched deflation, a weakening economy and a strong conviction in the market that things won't improve soon. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Skeptical On Shares, But Buying Anyway\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Foreign investors generally don't expect a dramatic turnaround in\par Japan's struggling corporate and banking sectors, but they are less\par worried about Japan than they were earlier in the year, when shares were\par falling to 20-year lows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now, scrambling to rebuild sharply underweight positions, foreign\par investors have been net buyers of Japanese shares for the past seven\par weeks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Goldman Sachs strategist Kathy Matsui predicts the Topix index could\par rise as high as 950, both because the government's rescue of Resona\par Holdings has calmed fears of a banking crisis, and because active\par monetary policy moves by a Bank of Japan led by new governor Toshihiko\par Fukui is leading to expectations for more aggressive measures.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cosmo Securities general manager Hiroshi Sato said by his calculations\par the fair value for the Nikkei is 9500, which it could top in September,\par adding that a "rise above 10000 wouldn't be surprising."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shares in Sony Corp. illustrate the turn to bullishness in the share\par market - they have jumped 30% from their lowest levels in April when the\par shares plunged due to its weak earnings and a grim forecast, in what the\par market dubbed the "Sony shock."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While many domestic investors remain skeptical that the sharp rises in\par the overall market will last, the mood is: "buy now anyway," a local\par trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "Deflationary Equilibrium"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's bond market has done even better than the stock market -\par yields have been tumbling to record lows almost daily - but for\par different reasons than equities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Paul Sheard, Lehman Brothers' chief economist for Asia, said bond\par market players are looking at Japan's economic situation and thinking:\par "Nothing has really changed. We're stuck in a deflationary equilibrium\par and this is going to go on for a long, long time."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That makes bonds an obvious place to park money for many domestic\par institutional investors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In spite of Japan's anemic economic growth and falling prices, foreign\par investors will likely keep putting more money back into Japanese stocks,\par if only to rebuild very oversold positions, strategists say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But foreign investors better be careful if they are betting that\par Japan's government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will stick to tough reform policies,\par the sort that would revive the economy and clean up the debt-clogged\par banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's unlikely there won't be a further round of disenchantment with\par policy and the economy, and that would be the trigger for the (stock)\par market to sell off again," Sheard said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par arran.scott@dowjones.com;\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Kanji Ishibashi, Kazuhiro Shimamura, Yoshio Takahashi and Asako\par Tanabe contributed to this report)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030611dz6b000oc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
