\par\par \b \uc2  Pound Falls After Report on Entering Euro Zone Released \b0\par\par\uc2 946 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2003\par 00:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar fell against most of its major rivals Monday but rose\par against the sterling, as Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown made\par clear that economic conditions weren't yet right for the United Kingdom\par to enter the euro zone.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the dollar was trading at 118.04 yen, down from 118.71 yen Friday. The euro was trading at $1.1739, up from $1.1689. \par \par \uc2  A mixed of news, including reports of Saudi Arabia buying huge sums of euros and a management shakeup at mortgage finance giant Freddie Mac held the dollar in check against the euro and yen, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  However, the pound took center stage with the release of a U.K. government-commissioned study that said sterling must fall by up to 16% for the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make economic sense for the U.K. \par \par \uc2  The study further reinforced a sense the government of British Prime Minister Tony Blair has embarked on a campaign to weaken the pound, which caught many market players off guard, sending sterling 2% lower against the euro and down 1.2% versus the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Further depreciation is now expected in the short term, as investors will be wary about holding on to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is being talked down, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, sterling was at $1.6440, down steeply from $1.6620 on Friday. The euro was fetching 0.7125 pound, off its intraday high of 0.7148 pound but up sharply from 0.7037 pound Friday. \par \par \uc2  While Mr. Brown's speech was explicitly about the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it represented yet another example of a rich nation engaging in actual or verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports amid sluggish global growth. \par \par \uc2  "It's an old trick; you export your unemployment to others while you kickstart your own economy," said Mark Austin, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at HSBC in London. \par \par \uc2  To varying degrees, the U.S., Japan, Switzerland and Norway are pursuing such policies. The only notable exception is the euro zone, where inflation-hawkish policymakers have expressed satisfaction at seeing the euro appreciate even though its strength continues to push the region into recession. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, President Bush weighed in on dollar policy again, reaffirming to reporters in Washington the U.S. commitment to a strong dollar and blaming interest-rate differentials for the greenback's weaker exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The greenback has sunk to multiyear lows against a slew of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the euro and sterling, in recent weeks amid comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow that the decline had been "modest" and could benefit U.S. exporters, which has lead market participants to second-guess the administration's professed dollar-friendliness. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuated on the morning's mixed bag of news, hopping within defined ranges, but generally under pressure from all its widely traded counterparts except the pound. Fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had helped cap yen appreciation in early European dealings, traders said. \par \par \uc2  News of a shakeup among top executives at Freddie Mac also put some short-lived pressure on the dollar as New York trading got underway. Foreign investors are significant holders of the agency's debt and a scandal could have sparked a massive selloff, analysts noted. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, reports say Saudi authorities have bought as much as 30 billion euros for dollars over the past six to eight weeks. \par \par \uc2  "They have been massive buyers of the euro against the dollar, and this is one of the reasons that it was so well supported on any dips," said a senior trader at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The same Saudi Arabian authorities have recently switched some of these holdings into sterling, said market participants in Asia and the Middle East. The euro-sterling sales started last week, accelerating Friday after Dow Jones Newswires reported on the recent activities and apparent plans of the Saudi Arabian Monetary Agency. \par \par \uc2  SAMA, which had kept a low profile in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the early 1990s, has been active since mid-April. But with its orders in the hands of just a few banks, the renewed official Saudi interest in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't widely known until now. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday, selling Turkish lira for dollars in a bid to halt the local unit's recent appreciation. The bank bought an estimated $500 million, a large sum for the local market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later said in a statement it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  after observing "extreme volatility." The bank maintains it has no exchange-rate target. \par \par \uc2  Local bankers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the lira hit 1.415 million Turkish lire to the dollar, its strongest level in a year. After the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in, the dollar strengthened to 1.44 million lire but was quoted at 1.431 million lire late in New York. \par \par \uc2  The lira has been appreciating against the dollar since the end of the U.S.-led war with Iraq, as market participants move out of cash dollar positions and into lira because of the high interest rate on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Local market participants said the dollar may decline again Tuesday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately to avoid signaling an exchange rate level. \par \par \uc2  Write to Michael Deibert at michael.deibert@dowjones.com and Adriana Arai at adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Benjamin Pedley in Singapore and Selim Atalay in Istanbul contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  06-09-03 1811ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030609dz690058x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
