\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves Jump, Reflecting  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Joseph Quini \par \uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2003\par 11:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves grew by the fastest pace ever to a record $543.09 billion in May, the government said Friday, reflecting the huge scale of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tokyo conducted last month to keep the yen from rising. \par \par \uc2  Foreign reserves increased by $43.65 billion, and the "main reason for the rise was obviously \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a ministry official briefing reporters on the data. \par \par \uc2  That sharp pace of increase was much faster than the $33-billion boost in foreign reserves in April 2000, when the Japanese government and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to keep the dollar above Y100. \par \par \uc2  Last month, the Japanese government spent massive amounts to prevent the yen from strengthening, after a series of remarks from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow prompted the market to think the U.S. was moving away from its long-held strong dollar policy. Japan fears a stronger yen would likely weigh on Japanese exports and worsen deflation. \par \par \uc2  Snow had said a weaker dollar would help U.S. exports and, while repeating that he favors a "strong" dollar, he pointedly declined to say this meant the greenback's exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged to a 27-month low of Y115.10 on May 19 before bouncing up Y2 on what was probably Tokyo's most furious burst of dollar-buying. \par \par \uc2  Friday's foreign reserves data came after recent BOJ data suggested Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities sold more than Y4.0 trillion in May, almost double the Y2.4 trillion it sold in the January-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  That brought the total amount of yen sold to keep the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak to a staggering Y6.4 trillion so far this year. \par \par \uc2  And top officials of Japan's Finance Ministry, which is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, warned Friday that the government is ready to order the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more if needed. \par \par \uc2  "It's like I've always said - we'll act as needed," said Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa at a news conference, in response to a question about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate moves. \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, a Shiokawa deputy and the vice finance minister for international affairs, said he was watching closely for excessive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around 0815 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y117.86, compared with\par Y117.71 late Thursday in New York and Y118.54 in Tokyo Thursday.\par    -By Joseph Quini, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par joseph.quini@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030606dz66000kn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
