\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: And Now The Big One: US Jobs \b0\par\par\uc2 1,549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2003\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Investors Shrug Off The Bad News\par DJIA          9041.30    gain 2.32         0.0%\par NASDAQ        1646.01    gain 11.36     up 0.7%\par S&P 500       990.14     gain 3.90      up 0.4%\par Dow Future    9045.00    gain 5.00      up 0.1%\par NASDAQ Future 1239.00    gain 5.00      up 0.4%\par S&P Future    991.75     gain 1.00      up 0.1%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.31% up 0.03\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Investors set aside disappointing employment data and lackluster retail sales reports Thursday, limiting their profit-taking in a market that eked out incremental gains and kept stocks at their highest levels in months. Treasurys rallied and then reversed. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Analysts said that despite the limited nature of Thursday's gains, the absence of a broader sell-off indicates a growing sentiment by investors that the market is rebounding. \par \par \uc2  "We've been in a long and deep bear market and investors feel like the worst is over, and what appeals to them more than anything is some of the stocks that have gotten hit the worst," said Richard E. Cripps, chief market strategist for Legg Mason of Baltimore. \par \par \uc2  "People are anticipating that the economy is going to continue to grow - slowly, but nonetheless we are coming up from a very depressed level," said Richard A. Dickson, senior market strategist at Lowry's Research Reports in Palm Beach, Fla. \par \par \uc2  Even so, investors remain cautious ahead of the government's release Friday of May jobless figures. \par \par \uc2  "You have to see strong numbers for us to be convinced that the economy is in a true comeback and not just going up on air," said Stephen Carl, principal and head of equity trading at The Williams Capital Group. \par \par \uc2  The Labor Department reported Thursday that the number of American workers filing new claims for jobless benefits climbed to a five-week high last week, indicating that companies are still contending with an economy that is struggling for footing. \par \par \uc2  Intel Corp. (INTC) Chief Financial Officer Andy Bryant said the company's second quarter is unfolding as expected with solid sales of computer chips and a weak flash chip business. \par \par \uc2  Bryant told analysts on a mid-quarter conference call that business is "performing in the forecast range" the company offered in April, when it projected revenue of $6.4 billion to $7 billion. Bryant narrowed that range Thursday to $6.6 billion to $6.8 billion. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: U.S. Treasury prices swung from opening strength to modest losses late Thursday, with the market consolidating ahead of Friday's employment report for May. \par \par \uc2  Early gains were powered by poor initial claims and factory orders data, with the long bond boosted by rate locks being unwound as the state of Illinois' $10 billion debt offering - containing a $7.65 billion 30-year tranche - was being priced. \par \par \uc2  At one stage the 10-year note yield made a 45-year low of 3.24% and the long bond yield reached 4.29%, before profit-taking emerged. \par \par \uc2  Now, the focus of the government bond market is Friday's key labor market report. Economists expect a decline of 25,000 jobs in May with the unemployment rate edging up to 6.1% from April's level of 6%. Complicating matters is the prospect of a benchmark statistical revision to the method of presenting the data. \par \par \uc2  "The Treasury market seems fairly comfortable at current levels ahead of payrolls," said Gerald Lucas, senior government bond strategist at Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Shares Drift Ahead Of U.S. Jobs Report\par USD-Yen             117.70       loss  0.05    dn 0.03%\par AUD-USD             0.6678       gain  0.0023  up 0.4%\par Nikkei 225          8672.86      gain  15.03   up 0.2%\par Hang Seng           9650.52      gain  11.51   up 0.1%\par JGB Yield           0.5000%      down 0.0150\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks drifted Friday morning as shares on\par Wall Street held their ground despite disappointing U.S. economic data.\par The dollar was lower against the yen following the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's short-term interest rate cut.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the stock market, investors took their lead off Wall Street's\par resilience, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Automakers Honda, Nissan and Toyota were among gainers Friday morning,\par but electronics giants Matsushita, NEC, Sony and Toshiba fell.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan will act in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if needed to ensure\par stability, though it isn't trying to manipulate the market, Japan's\par finance minister said Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's like I've always said - we'll act as needed," Masajuro Shiokawa\par told reporters.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shiokawa added that he believed the weakening of the dollar was\par related to the strengthening of the euro, rather than anything to do\par with the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spent Y4 trillion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen in May, a record\par amount for a single month. Officials have said they will continue to\par watch the market and act appropriately.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks Show Some Resilience\par Euro-USD            1.1852      gain  0.0010  up 0.09%\par Stlg-USD            1.6626      gain  0.0022  up 0.1%\par USD-Franc           1.3014      gain  0.0010  up 0.08%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European share prices should open slightly higher,\par with prices of government bonds and the euro little changed pending the\par U.S. labor figures.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European share prices are expected to open slightly higher and\par then hold until the U.S. jobs report at 1230 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks fell Thursday, despite a 50-basis-point cut in\par euro-zone interest rates, as investors focussed instead on\par weaker-than-expected U.S. data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts said the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's half-point reduction, which\par left its key refinancing rate at 2%, had been well flagged to the\par market. Most investors opted to book profits or "sell on the fact," they\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Simon Rubinsohn, economist at Gerrard, said the move was nevertheless\par welcome and should temporarily arrest the rise in the euro, which has\par compounded the existing pressure on euro-zone companies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "However, it is premature to conclude that this bold move will be\par sufficient to turn the economy around. We still expect a further cut in\par rates later in the year," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are likely to open little\par changed to slightly lower if investors elect to take some profit of\par Thursday's rally in heading into the weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Government bonds rallied Thursday after the 50-basis-points rate cut\par delivered by the ECB.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rally took flight when Wim Duisenberg, in his press conference\par following the ECB decision, omitted to say that rates were at an\par appropriate level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Duisenberg left the door open to further rate cut," said Vincent\par Chaigneau, fixed-income strategist with Societe Generale.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chaigneau said he was surprised by the strength of the market\par reaction, as the cut had been widely priced in.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After Duisenberg's speech, most market participants were persuaded\par that the ECB will continue to ease its monetary conditions, though not\par in the immediate future.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Rendez-vous in September," said Cyril Beuzit, fixed-income strategist\par with BNP Paribas.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session at around $1.1850, after a\par lackluster performance overnight in Asia, holding in a tight range after\par the dollar plummeted across the board Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The May U.S. jobs data Friday are likely to usher in more dollar\par weakness if, as market economists expect, they reflect further\par deterioration in the labor market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the ECB decision was largely priced into the market ahead of\par the meeting, the euro, which had already been rallying, skyrocketed. It\par hit a session high of $1.1886, within striking distance of its all-time\par record of $1.1930, before stabilizing and retracing a fraction of the\par day's gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In addition to the cut in rates, euro bulls were also cheered by the\par ECB's suggestion that it may ease again further down the road if need\par be.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We're seeing a continuation of the dollar weakness theme," said Larry\par Brickman, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with Bank of America Securities, noting\par Thursday's "weak data from the U.S. just puts more pressure on the\par dollar."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Friday, June 6: U.S. Labor Data\par ET/GMT\par 0100/0500 Japan        April Household Spending\par 0245/0645 France       End April Budget Balance\par 0515/0915 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  calls for bids\par 0830/1230 U.S.         May Nonfarm Payrolls (seen -20K)\par 0830/1230 U.S.         Jobless Rate (seen 6.1%, last 6.0%)\par 0940/1340 U.S.         May ECRI Inflation Gauge\par 1000/1400 Canada       May Ivey Purchasing Managers Index\par 1500/1900 U.S.         Apr Consumer Credit (seen +$2.5 Bln)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Regal Petroleum (U.RPT): FY Earnings\par Pretax loss (Reuters Reuters, 1 analysts): GBP0.36M (GBP0.7M)\par Forecast range: GBP0.36M - GBP0.36M\par Consensus sales: GBP1M (N/A)\par EasyJet (U.EZJ): May traffic figures\par Ingenta (U.IGA): 1H Earnings\par London Stock Exchange (U.LSE): May trading volumes\par Park Group (U.PKG): FY Earnings\par Ocean Wilson (U.OCN): AGM\par Independent News & Media (U.INW): AGM\par Ulster Television (U.UTV): AGM\par Clarins (F.CLR): AGM\par Oberthur Card System (F.OCS): AGM\par Coca-Cola HBC (I.COC): AGM\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 06-06-03 0500GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1011 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030606dz660008v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
