\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Shiokawa: Will Act In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Market If Needed \b0\par\par\uc2 182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2003\par 02:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan will act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed to ensure stability, though it isn't trying to manipulate the market, Japan's finance minister said Friday. \par \par \uc2  "It's like I've always said - we'll act as needed," Masajuro Shiokawa told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa added that he believed the weakening of the dollar was related to the strengthening of the euro, rather than anything to do with the yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent Y4 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in May, a record amount for a single month. Officials have said they will continue to watch the market and act appropriately. \par \par \uc2  At 0044 GMT, the dollar was changing hands at Y117.68, just down from Y117.71 in New York late Thursday. The dollar dived in the New York session against both the euro and the yen after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates by 50 basis points. \par \par \uc2  -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Bennett Richardson and Hugh Lawson \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030606dz6600018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
