\par\par \b \uc2  Yen up on pension transfer demand - ECB awaited. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2003\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 5 (Reuters) - The yen rose against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday on yen demand from Japanese investors preparing to transfer assets to state pension funds. \par \par \uc2  Among yen crosses, investors dumped the once high-flying euro on expectations of a hefty rate cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB), with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  extending losses to break the 138 yen mark and hit a two-week low of 137.72 yen. \par \par \uc2  That fall also put pressure on the euro against the dollar, prompting the single unit to shift closer to crucial support at $1.16. \par \par \uc2  "The market seems to have prepared for a possible rate cut of 50 basis point (by the ECB)," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at J.P. Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have been squaring positions ahead of the rate verdict as a majority of players are betting the current benchmark rate of 2.50 percent will be trimmed to 2.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  Tanase said a reduction of 50 basis points would take some shine off the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because interest rate differentials between the euro zone and the United States would narrow, while a cut of 25 basis points could support the euro. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. fed funds rate is now at 1.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  "From the stand point of interest rate differentials, a 25-basis point cut would still make the euro look good," Tanase said. \par \par \uc2  ECB President Wim Duisenberg and Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan both hinted at lower interest rates from their respective banks in remarks made on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The ECB rate announcement is due at 1145 GMT. \par \par \uc2  At 0537 GMT, the euro was trading at 138.28/35 yen bouncing from lows thanks to some short-covering. It was around 138.37 yen in late New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro stood at $1.1662/67 having slipped to a low of $1.1632. \par \par \uc2  PENSION FUNDS \par \par \uc2  Market participants said the yen received a lift in cross rates from Japanese trust banks seen managing corporate pension funds. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Japan's Health Ministry finalised rules covering the transfer of assets to the state pension fund from corporate schemes, which could affect 10 trillion yen ($84.15 billion) or more of assets. \par \par \uc2  It set September 1 as the date from which the handover would begin. \par \par \uc2  Once a transfer is approved by the authorities, corporate funds can hand back an amount set by the government in either cash or securities, but foreign securities need to be converted into cash. \par \par \uc2  Many Japanese firms have managed part of the public pension scheme for over 30 years, entrusted by the state with getting better returns for employees because of their supposed expertise. \par \par \uc2  But a decline in returns in general and the erosion of Japanese share prices in particular have forced a rethink. \par \par \uc2  "With the yen's recent falls against the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think many investors, who have been preparing to generate cash for the transfer of pension funds, are selling foreign stocks and bonds," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Still, the dollar's downside is firmly supported by fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with some dealers saying that the Japanese authorities had probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The latest warning against a strong yen came from Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi. \par \par \uc2  He said on Thursday that there was no reason for the yen to strengthen now and that the Japanese authorities would take necessary actions to keep exchange rates steady. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 118.54/62 yen compared with 118.81/89 yen in late New York trading. ($1=118.83 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030605dz6500d5j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
