\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS-Stocks surge on hopes of U.S. recovery. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Raju Gopalakrishnan \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2003\par 08:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 5 (Reuters) - Asian stocks surged across the board on Thursday, in tandem with gains on Wall Street, as a sharp expansion in the U.S. services sector persuaded investors a long-awaited economic rebound was in the offing. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Singapore, New Zealand, Thailand and the Philippines climbed to their highest levels of 2003 after data issued on Wednesday showed the U.S. services sector, which makes up 80 percent of the world's largest economy, grew for the second month in a row. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was stable against the yen and the euro as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets girded for what most expect will be a half percentage point cut in interest rates by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Oil slipped as fears of a supply crunch in the United States eased while safe haven assets gold and U.S. bonds also fell - bullion because of the stronger dollar and bond prices because of the expectations of economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Institute for Supply Management said its index of non-manufacturing activity rose to 54.5 in May from 50.7 in April, easily beating forecasts of 52.0. \par \par \uc2  The data led the Dow Jones Industrial Average up 1.3 percent to its highest close since August. Markets in Asia's export-dependent economies were quick to follow suit. \par \par \uc2  "Any story on the U.S. recovery is a boon to a country like Korea because its exports heavily depend on the U.S. market," said Cho Yong-chan, an analyst at Daishin Securities Co in Seoul, where the index closed 0.33 percent stronger. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Nikkei average closed 1.16 percent up at 8,657.23, a three-and-a-half-month high. Singapore was up 1.8 percent at its highest since November, while Hong Kong gained about one percent, Bombay surged 1.5 percent and Taiwan closed 1.16 percent up. \par \par \uc2  UNBELIEVABLE RISE \par \par \uc2  Shares in the Philippines closed almost four percent up at an 11-month high on hopes for an easing of monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "It's unbelievable. I never thought the market would kick up this much," said A&A Securities research head Astro del Castillo. \par \par \uc2  "I guess the low inflation picture raised hopes of lower interest rates ahead. Players also tracked the bullish trend in the United States." \par \par \uc2  The MSCI Asia Pacific Free Index ex-Japan, a broad measure of regional markets outside Japan, was up 0.9 percent at 170.39 points, its highest since August. \par \par \uc2  Technology stocks led the rises in Asia, but banks and other services-oriented firms also gained. \par \par \uc2  "First, techs were in vogue but the buying focus is shifting to banks and others, which shows that sentiment is good and the current uptrend intact," said Koji Muneoka, head of domestic sales trading at HSBC Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Banks are not rising on any particular factor. Investors are buying Japan and banks are now benefiting from that trend." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets took a back seat to the action in equities, awaiting the ECB meeting at 1145 GMT that is almost certain to deliver a rate cut. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 118.60 yen compared with 118.85 yen in late New York trading, sandwiched by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and yen-buying by Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1661, little changed from late New York, and dealers said a 50 basis point ECB cut had been factored in. \par \par \uc2  Front-end oil futures were at $29.82 per barrel, pushed down 18 cents from Wednesday by a boost in U.S. oil inventories which eased fears of a summer supply crunch. \par \par \uc2  Gold was last at $361.60, down about $1 from Wednesday because of the stronger dollar. The yield on the benchmark U.S. 10-year note was at about 3.32 percent, off a 45-year low of 3.28 percent on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030605dz65006bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
