\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro stumbles as aggressive ECB rate cut eyed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2003\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 4 (Reuters) - The euro fell one percent against the yen and hovered two cents below recent record highs against the dollar on Wednesday as dealers bet on an aggressive interest rate cut from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the following day. \par \par \uc2  Comments from ECB President Wim Duisenberg on Tuesday that falling oil prices and a strong euro had lowered inflationary pressure tipped the balance in favour of an aggressive 50 basis point cut, undermining the euro's yield appeal. \par \par \uc2  However, with Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan also hinting at lower U.S. rates on Tuesday, the yen proved the biggest beneficiary. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people who went long of euros against the yen are locking in profits ahead of the ECB," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  The euro was holding just above session lows at 138.85 yen in the European midsession, more than two yen below last Friday's record high. It traded with a soft bias against the dollar at $1.1710 but well within recent ranges. \par \par \uc2  "Two counteracting forces are at work, which is leaving euro/dollar in limbo," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  "The market has moved closer to pricing in a 50 basis point cut from the ECB but is also increasingly expecting a cut from the Fed." \par \par \uc2  RATE CUT PRESSURE \par \par \uc2  Pressure for lower rates in the euro zone intensified after data showed retail sales in the 12-nation bloc posted their biggest monthly decline in nearly a year in March. \par \par \uc2  The retail sales figures followed data earlier in the day showing the euro zone's service sector continued to contract in May as a hoped-for bounce in the economy after the Iraq war failed to materialise. \par \par \uc2  "The major focus for now is tomorrow's ECB, where most people are expecting a 50 basis point cut," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank. \par \par \uc2  The Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also meeting on Wednesday, is widely expected to announce a cut on Thursday of as much as half a percentage point. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England starts a two-day policy meeting on Wednesday and is expected to leave British interest rates steady at 3.75 percent on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Fed's next interest rate meeting is June 24-25. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was down half a percent at 118.65 yen, nursing losses despite more efforts by Japanese officials to talk their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Hiroshi Watanabe said on Wednesday that authorities would stay vigilant against speculative moves that push the yen higher. \par \par \uc2  Data last Friday showed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted the largest ever monthly volume of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May, totalling some four trillion yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030604dz6400n01\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
