\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar stuck as Bush remarks at G8 fail to impress. \b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2003\par 01:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 3 (Reuters) - The dollar failed to make headway against the euro or yen on Tuesday in a market unimpressed by President George W. Bush's indication of his support for a strong dollar at the G8 summit in France. \par \par \uc2  Late on Monday, a senior U.S. official said Bush told other Group of Eight leaders that he favoured a strong dollar and that U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged. \par \par \uc2  But the comment was seen giving little incentive to Tokyo dealers to push up the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "President Bush's comments (on a strong dollar) haven't changed since his inauguration," said Hidehiko Inamura, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had factored in prospects of a discussion on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates at the summit as some leaders had already shown concern about the dollar's fall and hinted that the topic would be on the agenda. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has slid more than 10 percent to all-time lows against the euro this year. Against the yen it is in line with its rate at the start of the year after falling to 115.10 yen in mid-May. \par \par \uc2  "The market is reacting little to Bush's comments," said a dealer at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Few dealers take Bush's comments at face value and many say the U.S. administration will probably take a non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2 , laissez-faire approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  "What he really means with his 'strong dollar' policy is that the government will let the market decide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. If U.S. economic fundamentals are strong the dollar will strengthen, and if they're weak the dollar will weaken," he added. \par \par \uc2  At 2314 GMT, the dollar was trading at 118.70/75 yen compared with 118.62/70 in late New York. It is expected to trade in a range of 118.20-119.20 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at $1.1743/47 against $1.1756/62 in late U.S. trading. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unchanged from late New York levels at 139.33/74 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders do not expect the dollar to break out of its trading range against the yen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair is caught between fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the downside and exporters' selling on the upside. \par \par \uc2  For the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market is focused on Thursday's rate-setting meeting by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers are still split between a 25 basis point cut and a 50 basis point cut from the current benchmark refinancing rate at 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030602dz62018wr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
