\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Have Legs After All \b0\par\par\uc2 1,568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2003\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Wall Street Rallies On Economic Data\par DJIA          8850.26    gain 139.08    up 1.6%\par NASDAQ        1595.91    gain 20.96     up 1.3%\par S&P 500       963.59     gain 13.95     up 1.5%\par Dow Future    8870.00    gain 139.99    up 1.6%\par NASDAQ Future 1207.50    gain 8.50      up 0.7%\par S&P Future    968.75     gain 5.50      up 0.6%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.42% up 0.08\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Wall Street notched its third straight month of gains Friday - a feat not seen in nearly two years - after investors cheered a pair of reports showing a rise in consumer sentiment and Midwest manufacturing. Prices of Treasurys were lower. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: As the markets gain technical momentum, economic figures are adding to the view that stocks should climb more. \par \par \uc2  "We have more positive economic data that's got investors heartened the economy is continuing to improve," said Stephen Massocca, president of Pacific Growth Equities. "Investors are pretty optimistic." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve Board Governor Ben Bernanke said Saturday that there is no immediate threat of deflation in the U.S., but that monetary policy is aimed at stopping inflation from becoming uncomfortably low. \par \par \uc2  "There is no immediate threat of deflation in the U.S. economy," he told reporters after giving a speech to a monetary policy conference in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "We want to insure inflation doesn't fall further, not because we fear imminent deflation, but because we want inflation to stay within the appropriate range, which...in my case is 1% to 2%," he said. \par \par \uc2  On the economy, Bernanke said he is optimistic given supportive monetary and fiscal policy, falling oil prices and strong consumer sentiment and productivity. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: Treasury prices lost ground Friday on improving economic figures. \par \par \uc2  The strong Chicago PMI was the most surprising to the market. The Chicago survey is watched closely for clues about the state of national manufacturing activity, as reported by the Institute for Supply Management. The ISM May survey will be released Monday at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "We expect a small increase in the ISM manufacturing index for May, reported next Monday," said Peter Kretzmer, senior economist at Banc of America Securities in New York. "However, it likely remained below-50 for a third consecutive month," he added. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tech Shares Fuel Tokyo Rally\par USD-Yen             119.12       loss  0.46    dn 0.4%\par AUD-USD             0.6505       loss  0.0003  dn 0.05%\par Nikkei 225          8601.29      gain  176.70  up 2.1%\par Hang Seng           9606.74      gain  119.30  up 1.3%\par JGB Yield           0.5250% down 0.0050\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks soared 2% Monday on fresh gains in New\par York and a recovering dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said technology stocks were racking up most of the gains, with\par Sony, Sanyo, Hitachi and Toshiba among the morning winners.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market watchers added, however, that some exporting blue-chip\par companies were slipping on profit-taking after hefty gains last week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the benchmark 10-year bond was 0.5250% early Monday, down\par from 0.5300% Friday. pressures in Japan's economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Federal Reserve Board Governor Ben Bernanke indicated he thought\par heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's authorities to\par stem the yen's gains versus the dollar will have only a temporary\par effect.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Data issued by the Bank of Japan Friday indicate Japan spent a record\par Y3.983 trillion to stop the dollar falling sharply against the yen in\par May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bernanke said the best way for Japan to end deflation is for the Bank\par of Japan and government to temporarily coordinate policy and\par aggressively ease policy settings.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said the BOJ should agree to buy a large amount of Japanese\par government bonds to help the government fund large tax cuts to consumers\par and businesses to jump start the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks Expected To Start Week Higher\par Euro-USD            1.1719      gain  0.0001  up 0.01%\par Stlg-USD            1.6290      gain  0.0016  up 0.07%\par USD-Franc           1.3035      gain  0.0049  up 0.3%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European share prices should open higher, while\par prices of government bonds and the euro start at lower levels.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares should start the session with considerable\par momentum from Wall Street and a lower euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Munich Re heads the corporate results list, and a pleasant surprise in\par its report of first-quarter earnings could add more fuel to the rebound\par in the insurance sector.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In corporate news, scientists reported Genentech's Avastin and\par ImClone's Erbitux provided substantial benefits in treating colon-cancer\par patients. The two new drugs work by directly attacking the genetic\par mutations in tumors. Genentech is a unit of Roche Holding.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are expected to continue their\par correction, with the euro lower and the prospects for German structural\par reforms increasing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder gained resounding support from his party\par Sunday for a reform package aimed at restarting the stalled German\par economy, an important first step for a plan that he claimed would help\par lighten Europe's economic gloom.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bond prices were lower Friday, after data showed\par growth in the U.S. manufacturing sector. Several European purchasing\par managers reports are due Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session just over $1.1700 as it tries to\par regain momentum in the face of a bullish Wall Street outlook. Support at\par around $1.1655 provides some good short-run chart support.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In general, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets look poised for volatile trading this\par week, with a slew of announcements expected from central banks and a\par flood of U.S. and European economic data likely to influence the\par dollar's performance.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While analysts generally believe that the long-term trend still favors\par a decline for the dollar and an advance for the common European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , many feel the euro's march upward could be far less strident\par than it has been in recent weeks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A heavy week of U.S. data culminates this Friday with the release of\par nonfarm payrolls and the unemployment rate for May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The indicators in the U.S., one step at a time, are lining up to\par stronger growth," said Ram Bhagavataula, chief economist with Royal Bank\par of Scotland in New York. "I'm expecting over the next two to four months\par for the dollar to hold its own."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moreover, the rally in the U.S. stock market may become convincing\par enough that it can no longer be ignored by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Comments by President George W. Bush reaffirmed the U.S. commitment to\par a strong dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With the euro-zone economy in the doldrums, most analysts anticipate\par the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will cut interest rates at its meeting\par Thursday, with estimates of the size of the cut having been rolled up to\par 50 basis points from a previous consensus of 25 basis points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A cut of 50 basis points would reduce the ECB's benchmark rate to 2.0%\par compared with 1.25% for its U.S. equivalent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And attention will focus on U.S. and European economic reports, as\par well as remarks from top G8 political and monetary officials on the\par dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tuesday, representatives of the world's leading central banks gather\par in Berlin for a three-day international monetary conference. Federal\par Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan is scheduled to participate in a panel\par discussion via satellite transmission from Washington.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A widely watched survey of euro-zone industry purchasing managers is\par due out early Monday and is expected to remain below 50 - indicating a\par contraction - for the ninth month running.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Monday, June 2: Purchasing Managers\par ET/GMT\par 0100/0500 Japan        May Auto Sales (last -6.5% YY)\par 0400/0800 France       Purchasing Managers Manufacturing\par 0300/0700 Italy        State Sector Borrowing Requirement\par 0430/0830 U.K.         Purchasing Managers Manufacturing\par 0430/0830 France       May New Vehicle Registrations\par 0900/1300 France       Weekly T-bill Auction Results\par 0930/1330 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  calls for bids\par 1000/1400 U.S.         April Construction Spending\par 1000/1400 U.S.         May ISM Manufacturing\par 1530/1930 U.S.         San Fransisco Fed President Parry\par    N/A    U.S.         Group of Eight three-day summit\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Munich Re (G.MUV): 1Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 6 analysts): EUR62M (EUR4.48B)\par Consensus gross premiums: EUR11.1B (EUR10.7B)\par Fountains (U.FNS): FY Earnings\par Note: The company said in Nov '02 that pretax profit for the year to\par Sep. 30 was GBP1.025B compared to GBP945M in the previous year.\par Laurent Perrier (F.LPR): FY Earnings\par Note: Reported revenue of EUR162.8M in May up 7.9% from EUR150.8M the\par year before.\par ACAL (U.ACL): FY Earnings\par Pretax profit (Reuters Research, 1 analyst): GBP14.5M (GBP16.6M)\par Consensus sales: GBP276M (GBP297M)\par Maelor (U.MAL): FY Earnings\par Pretax profit (Reuters Research, 1 analyst): GBP0.1M (GBP0.9M)\par Christian Salvesen (U.SVC): FY Earnings\par Note: The European logistics company said in Feb that it expects\par profits for the year to March 31 to be 20% below current market\par expectations.\par Dawson Holdings (U.DHD): 1H Earnings\par Corporacion Mapfre (E.MPF): To Pay Extra 2002 EUR0.10 Dividend\par Metrovacesa (E.MTV): EGM\par Fimalac (F.FMA): AGM\par Surf Control (U.SRF): AGM\par Zoo Digital (U.ZOO): AGM/EGM\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 02-06-03 0500GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1011 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030602dz620008q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
