\par\par \b \uc2  Bush Strong Dollar Comments Ring Hollow To  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,032 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2003\par 21:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- President George W. Bush may have thrown a symbolic bone to his Group of Eight counterparts over the weekend by backing a strong dollar more clearly than his administration has in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  But while his remarks certainly sparked some scrambling among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market investors, most agree Bush hasn't taken from the notion of unofficial U.S. support for a declining dollar as long as it remains gradual, a view shared by the foremost spokesman on the dollar, Treasury Secretary John Snow. \par \par \uc2  "In a way it's good cop-bad cop and Bush is the good cop," said Rebecca Patterson, a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan, in New York. "He's gone to France and is trying to mollify policy makers who are nervous about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. But he's not trying to change the dollar's directions." \par \par \uc2  Speaking in France two weeks ago, Snow - the bad cop in this particular allusion - sent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders scrambling after he said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were "really fairly modest realignments of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," indicating he saw further room for the dollar to fall. \par \par \uc2  Those comments had an explosive effect, ultimately helping the euro leap to a record high of $1.1930 and underscoring a sense that the U.S. had effectively dismantled the strong dollar policy as it had been known. \par \par \uc2  Perhaps mindful of this, Bush said over the weekend that "The market, at this point in time, has devalued the dollar, which is contrary to our policy," a comment that helped propel the dollar sharply higher during Asian and European trading. The euro fell as low as $1.1655 at one point, from $1.1775 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  At first glance, Bush's statement seemed like an emphatic endorsement of the strong dollar policy initiated by Clinton-era Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin. But a closer reading of other Bush comments firmed a sense, as Monday's trading wore on, that the U.S. hasn't changed tack in any way, nor does it plan to take the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  route and start actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Market participants paid particular attention to this Bush remark: "We believe that good fiscal and monetary policy will cause our economy to grow and that the marketplace will see a growing economy and therefore strengthen the dollar." \par \par \uc2  "What he is saying is it is still up to the markets," said Patterson at JP Morgan, adding that broad market sentiment also holds that the dollar will do better once the U.S. economy starts to recover in earnest. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par       Looking For A Gradual Move\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For now though, many in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and fixed income markets\par think the dollar should be weaker, as it adjusts from the period during\par which it was severely overvalued in the late 1990s. Reflecting this, the\par dollar started to pare its gains across the board in New York trading,\par with the euro trading around $1.1760 late in the day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a roundabout way, Bush has assured, if anything, that further\par adjustment in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets will remain fairly  gradual, which should\par continue to help U.S. exporters by making their products more\par competitive abroad and helping to lift the U.S. economy ahead of the\par 2004 presidential elections.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The unfortunate thing is they're in a bit of a box," said Marcel\par Kasumovich, head of G-10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York.\par "They've made this prior commitment to a strong dollar policy, but now\par all of a sudden there's an asymmetry. If they say they're not committed,\par then you get the risk of a disorderly decline in the dollar. If they say\par they are, they can see the adjustment we've seen continuing."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As evidenced in the flood of remarks from top G8 officials out of\par Evian on Monday - most of which either welcomed Bush's commitment to a\par strong dollar or expressed their own support for a strong dollar - such\par diplomatic reassurances on the part of the U.S. appeared to help smooth\par frayed economic nerves among many key trading partners.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of course, the irony of the whole `strong dollar policy' is that\par there's no real policy behind it. Unlike Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par which allegedly spent around $34 billion last month buying dollars for\par yen in an attempt to support the flagging U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , nobody expects\par the U.S. to take concrete action to manipulate exchange rates one way or\par another.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This is partly because of a traditional abhorrence among U.S. policy\par makers to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a policy tool. But strategists believe it's\par also due to the fact that in unloading so many dollars on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is effectively carrying out all the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the\par market might need, helping to stabilize the dollar and ensure it doesn't\par fall entirely off a cliff. And despite the U.S. opposition to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , analysts believe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's open market tactics must enjoy\par some sort of quiet approval from the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On many fronts, given the frequency with which U.S. officials speak on\par the `strong dollar policy', it's surprising the statements continue to\par have such a market impact. Unlike in Japan where a long tradition of\par talking down the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has naturally diminished the influence of any\par comments, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets pounce on statements from U.S.\par officials and appear to have an insatiable appetite for verbal analysis\par from the administration on exchange rate policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But in most cases - and Monday's experience was no exception - the\par U.S. jawboning turned out to be nothing more than a short-term trading\par opportunity for a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market preoccupied with real issues, such as\par a likely European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate cut this week or when the Bank of\par Japan might step into the market again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If you look at broader macroeconomic dynamics, the comments are just\par a blunt instrument," said Kasumovich. "They can have a shorter-term\par influence on the market, but that's about it."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381;\par grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030602dz6200ane\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
