\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rallies on U.S. data, eyes G8 meeting. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2003\par 22:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 30 (Reuters) - The dollar rose sharply on Friday in a broad-based move, bolstered by a strong regional manufacturing report that sparked optimism among investors that the U.S. economy could be moving toward sturdier growth. \par \par \uc2  Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's advance, some analysts were skeptical the dollar will sustain its gains, especially if U.S. data next week including the national Institute for Supply Management report prove soft. Economists expect that report, for release on Monday, to show a reading of 48.6 in May, up from 45.4 in April. \par \par \uc2  "To some degree, the latest bout of economic growth (as indicated by Friday's reports) won't really change the interest rate environment a whole lot, nor will it change the backdrop for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2  Although a rise in U.S. consumer sentiment, driven by rising stock prices and fading concerns over the war in Iraq contributed to the dollar's rise on Friday, the greenback is still probing record lows against the euro and multi-year troughs against a host of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The fact that three heads of state chose to speak about that decline before a meeting of the world's richest nations impelled investors to buy the dollar on the unlikely chance of an initiative to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Group of Eight leaders gather in France on Sunday. On Friday, rare dollar comments from U.S. President George W. Bush, Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi and German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder appeared to heighten concern about the dollar's 13 percent fall against the euro this year. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES: ON THE AGENDA \par \par \uc2  "This represents a growing event risk ahead of the G8 Summit, sending a signal to the market that exchange rates will be discussed. While the market doesn't expect any substantive action, I think people are playing it safe and that has propelled the dollar higher," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Ruesch International in Washington. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's biggest surge on Friday happened after the May Chicago Purchasing Management Index, a barometer for manufacturing activity in the U.S. Midwest, rose to 52.2 from 47.6 in April, jumping above the 50 mark, signaling expansion. \par \par \uc2  "The markets were surprised at the strength in the Chicago Purchasing Management survey. It indicates that the U.S. economy may be doing slightly better than the markets had anticipated and the euro reacted by selling off in a pretty strong reaction to the number," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Putnam Investments in Boston. \par \par \uc2  The euro slumped to a low of $1.1738, down more than 1 percent in value before climbing back to $1.1769, down 1.1 percent on the session in late New York trade. The euro is well below Tuesday's record high of $1.1932. \par \par \uc2  Backing up the manufacturing report was a rise in the University of Michigan's consumer sentiment index for May. The index rose to 92.1 from 86.0 in April, the highest point since the June 2002 reading of 92.4. \par \par \uc2  "Over the long term this dollar rally is not likely to be sustained and the only way for the dollar to make a significant recovery is for consistent improvement and we have not seen that yet," said Beuzelin. \par \par \uc2  The dollar climbed to 119.63 yen, a one month high, before slipping back to 119.31 yen, still up 1.2 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials confirmed on Friday the government sold 3.9825 trillion yen ($33.70 billion) in May - a record single month figure - after refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to maintain a weak yen in order to protect its fragile export sector, one of only a few bright spots in an otherwise dismal economy. \par \par \uc2  Traders are expecting the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which next meets on June 5, to ease monetary policy in response to a sluggish economy. \par \par \uc2  A cut in the benchmark lending rate of 2.50 percent would reduce the interest rate advantage euro-based assets have over dollar-denominated assets, where the benchmark rate is 1.25 percent. However, the record U.S. current account deficit is expected to remain an overbearing drain on the dollar's value for the foreseeable future. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 1.2998 francs against the Swiss franc, up 1.3 percent, but still within sight of a 6-1/2-year low of 1.2779 francs hit on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell against the dollar from four-month highs to trade at $1.6352, down 1.1 percent. (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030530dz5u0137a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
