\par\par \b \uc2  Fresh Thinking Unlikely To Spring From G8 Alpine Summit \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,170 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2003\par 14:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Brian Blackstone \par \par \uc2  PARIS -(Dow Jones)- If economist George Magnus had his way the world's biggest economies would use their weekend summit in the French Alps to call the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to order - throw a crutch to the stumbling U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Their pronouncements would go down in history as the "Evian Accords". They'd be poured over by economics students two decades hence, just like the Plaza and Louvre Accords that once brought stability back to chaotic foreign exchanges. \par \par \uc2  "If you could design an agenda issue of maximum importance for the global economy, you'd want a Plaza II (type of) agreement" to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, said UBS Warburg's Magnus. \par \par \uc2  In reality, though, he knows only too well that Evian, the resort which is hosting the Group of Eight meetings Sunday through Tuesday, will never be a source of fresh economic thought - just bottles of pure, spring water. \par \par \uc2  French President Jacques Chirac has made that all too clear. He has set the bar fairly low, voicing the hope that a message of confidence in the global economy's prospects will emerge, despite volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and a lack of structural reform that cry out for united action. \par \par \uc2  The 1985 Plaza Accords were aimed at lowering what was seen as an overvalued U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now the problem is the exact opposite, for the dollar has slid 27% against the euro and 5% against the yen in the past 12 months. \par \par \uc2  Though officials have stressed that the greenback is still near historical norms, the speed of its fall has proved a headache for European multinationals and exporters and a windfall for corporate America. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Conflicting Signals\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Magnus's imaginary world of the "Evian Accords" President Bush\par agrees on joint action to stem the dollar's slide and horsetrades to get\par Europe and Japan to agree reforms to pensions, labor markets and fiscal\par policy in order to boost their own long-term growth prospects.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet Chirac has already dismissed the idea of any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par pronouncements. "I don't think the present situation requires any\par particular comment," he is quoted by Monday's Financial Times as having\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Friday, Bush was banging the drum for the dollar, saying in an\par interview with France's Le Figaro that U.S. economic policies support a\par strong U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Yet his comments offered no relief, failing to\par dislodge the euro far from its four-year high just under $1.19.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Markets prefer to believe Bush's Treasury Secretary, John Snow, who\par two weeks ago said the dollar's tumble has been "fairly modest" - proof,\par as they see it, of an acceptance by the administration of a weaker\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thus, the U.S. position is a mess. Without a stronger stance on\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , "the U.S. (dollar) will stay mired in correction" and\par markets will focus more on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meetings next week, especially\par the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's on June 5, said Marc Chandler, chief\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at HSBC in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The G8 includes the world's leading industrialized nations - the U.S.,\par Japan, Germany, the U.K., France, Italy and Canada - as well as Russia.\par But Chirac has effectively turned the event into a mini United Nations\par by sending invitations to the heads of China, Brazil, Egypt, Saudi\par Arabia, Senegal and many other developing countries, pushing the\par percentage of world gross domestic product represented at the meetings\par to 75%, versus the 50% accounted for by the G8 alone.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sunday's meetings will include emerging economies, while Monday's and\par Tuesday's are reserved for members of the G8 club, whose leaders will\par discuss economics, trade, the recent better mood in financial markets\par and how to tackle financial crises.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And with highly-publicized diplomatic rows pitting the U.S. and U.K.\par against France and Germany over Iraq, Evian is as much an opportunity\par for a cozy, family snapshot as any policy pronouncements. Chirac and\par Bush have a tete-a-tete Monday before Bush leaves early to begin a\par Mideast tour.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    Prayer For Guidance\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against all hope, Christa Randzio-Plath, chair of the European\par Parliament's Economic and Monetary Affairs Committee, is calling for\par hands-on economic measures from the G8 to get Europe's most troubled\par economies such as Germany on the move again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The G8 should return to its origin in coordinating macroeconomic\par policies in a world of uncertainty."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par She wants leaders to come up with "stimulative budgetary policies" as\par well as easier monetary policy in Europe.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro-zone economy stalled during the first quarter, as did Japan,\par and both are expected to record minimal growth this year. The U.S. fared\par slightly better, growing at a 1.9% pace in the three months to\par end-March, but is still operating well below potential.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But this isn't the 1980s. Much of the euro zone's fiscal and monetary\par policy has been ceded to the E.U. in Brussels or the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Frankfurt, both of which vigilantly guard their independence\par from national politicians.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's decade-long economic malaise has made it a secondary player in\par global economic policy despite being the second-biggest G8 economy.\par Take, for instance, Tokyo's near-impotence in its efforts to stem the\par yen's rise against the dollar despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's left Bush in the politically enviable position of being able to\par deliver stimulus to the world's largest economy. Free of the type of\par fiscal constraints imposed in Europe, he recently signed a $350 billion\par tax-cut package. The Federal Reserve, for its part, has kept interest\par rates at four-decade lows, half the ECB rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     Faint Praise For G8\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bush even comes to Evian's overwhelmingly political stage armed with a\par dramatic pledge to devote $15 billion to fight AIDS in Africa, leaving\par Chirac, whose spokeswoman said Africa "must remain a priority" for the\par G8, faced with calls to match the U.S. president's thunder.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the U.S. can't continue pumping money into places like Africa and\par the Middle East while delivering tax cuts to Americans without the\par say-so of investors in Europe and Asia. They have to continue buying\par U.S. debt, thus financing Washington's vast current account deficit,\par running at some $500 billion a year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's why broader, more balanced growth across the globe is in Bush's\par interest as much as anyone else's.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The trouble is that a G8 summit can't serve as tool for the kind of\par economic fine-tuning delivered by landmark agreements such as Plaza.\par Still, Chandler contends, it's the best forum for tackling contemporary\par ills like terrorism financing, money laundering and banking reform.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The G8 helps facilitate" action in these areas, he said. "We're\par marginally better off with the G8" than without.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Brian Blackstone, Dow Jones Newswires; +33 1 4017 1740;\par brian.blackstone@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030530dz5u000tj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
