\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr/Yen Up As Japan Jawbones, Investors Buy \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2003\par 07:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar rose against the yen in Asia Friday as the Japanese government stepped up its verbal assault on its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japanese investors took notice. \par \par \uc2  Government officials including Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi and Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa issued warnings aimed at curbing the resurgent yen. \par \par \uc2  "Given the current state of the economy, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be weaker," Koizumi said Thursday in a group interview with European media, referring to recent data showing that gross domestic product growth fell to almost zero in the January-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  Koizumi's remarks, reported in the Financial Times international edition Friday, kept market players on the alert for potential covert yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa echoed his leader's concerns by saying the yen is currently a bit too strong. \par \par \uc2  "Those comments helped to spark some dollar short-covering by interbank players this morning," said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Interbank-led buybacks, month-end dollar demand at Tokyo retail 0100 GMT fixing and healthy buying of U.S., Australian and Canadian units by Japanese securities houses to set up foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investment trust funds lifted the dollar to an intraday high of Y118.49. \par \par \uc2  But Japanese exporters soon took advantage of the dollar's rise against the yen to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, selling the greenback down to the low-Y118s. \par \par \uc2  At 0537 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y118.35, above Y118.01 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y118.73 late Thursday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1876, lower than $1.1901 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.1764 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y140.51, up from Y140.44 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y139.67 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese and U.S. officials have provided verbal support to the dollar after comments earlier this month from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow sent it into freefall. \par \par \uc2  Even President George W. Bush joined the fray, saying late Thursday that he will assure the Group of Eight leading nations he backs a strong U.S. dollar when he meets them at the G8 summit in Evian, France this weekend. \par \par \uc2  "I will reiterate our strong-dollar policy," Bush said during an interview with a handful of foreign news organizations. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said the recent remarks from Bush and Koizumi suggest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be discussed at the G8 summit. \par \par \uc2  "That's an issue which the markets will have to keep a close eye out for," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Later in the session, players will also be watching the release of Bank of Japan data on the sources of change in its monthly current account, which will effectively confirm any recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted by the MOF, by revealing the amount of money the government has spent in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this month. \par \par \uc2  In May, Japan's Finance Ministry is estimated to have spent more than Y4 trillion on its covert yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to a fresh record high of Y140.68 in the Asian session, pulled up by the rise of the U.S., Canadian and Australian dollars against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6508, down from $1.6521 in New York but higher than $1.6375 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Bennett Richardson \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030530dz5u000a6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
