\par\par \b \uc2  POINT OF VIEW: Botswana's Not So Bad Now Mr Koizumi? \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2003\par 21:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- It's never been a secret that Japan's government will go whatever extra mile it can to persuade \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to sell the yen. Recent remarks by premier Junichiro Koizumi take the biscuit. \par \par \uc2  Heading to the 300th anniversary celebrations in the Russian city of St. Petersburg, on his way to the Evian summit of the Group of Eight, Koizumi told reporters the yen was too strong given the current state of the world's second largest economy. A fairly innocuous statement given the deeply-entrenched tradition among Japanese policy makers of trying to talk the yen lower - a practice affectionately referred to in the market as 'jawboning.' \par \par \uc2  But what was more surprising was his next statement: "Credit rating agencies have downgraded Japanese government bonds to below those of Botswana. Yet why doesn't the Japanese yen depreciate?," Koizumi asked, according to the Financial Times. \par \par \uc2  Of course a year can be a long time in politics, but it was only last June, after Moody's Ratings Agency submitted Japan to the humiliation of a two-notch downgrade in its sovereign credit rating, that some of the people in Koizumi's cabinet practically exploded. \par \par \uc2  Enraged at being ranked below some developing countries - the Botswana comparison in particular seemed to add to public outrage in Japan - the country's then top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, Haruhiko Kuroda, took the unusual step of writing letters of complaint to Moody's, along with another one to Standard & Poor's, which had also downgraded Japan. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda argued the ratings agencies - Moody's, S&P and Fitch - were ignoring real strengths, such as Japan's huge personal savings, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and current account surplus in assessing the nation's credit risk. \par \par \uc2  And he used strong language, saying the downgrades weren't reasonable or warranted. In Moody's case, which hurt the most because of the double whammy nature of the downgrade, Kuroda sought to argue the ratings agency was just plain wrong. \par \par \uc2  Well, if Koizumi's comments now can provide any guidance, it is perhaps this: while Japan may not have liked the downgrades at the time, it's happy to highlight them as part of its constantly expanding box of tools to influence the exchange rate of the yen. It's like saying "look how poorly we are being perceived, why are you still propping up our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?" \par \par \uc2  More than the talk, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's open market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics are certainly reaping a large measure of success. Desperate to stop the strong yen from derailing what's been a very fragile, export-led recovery, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - acting via the Bank of Japan - has allegedly spent a whopping $34 billion buying dollars for yen in May alone, bringing its year-to-date \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tally to about $55 billion. \par \par \uc2  Stepping covertly into the market, the Japanese authorities - by far the most ardent practitioners of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the industrialized world - have succeeded in injecting an element of doubt into traders' minds, while clearing out many speculators, those investors who take a short term view of the market and often tend to vamp up market volatility. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the dollar has held remarkably steady against the yen, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the grip of a major realignment, has gyrated massively against other rivals, in particular the euro. Consider these numbers: the dollar has actually increased 0.5% against the yen since the beginning of the year, while the euro has appreciated 13% against the dollar. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also fallen about 13% this year against the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Clearly, the yen is still too strong for Koizumi and the rest of the Japanese government, even though his spokesman was quick to deny that the prime minister was seeking to actively guide the yen lower, stressing he was "just commenting on the facts." \par \par \uc2  Well, the facts are that Japan is putting its money where its mouth is and it's working. Maybe the belated embracing of the Botswana allusion will only help that money go a little bit further. \par \par \uc2  (Grainne McCarthy, is Assistant Managing Editor for fixed income, macroeconomics and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Grainne has also reported for Dow Jones Newswires in Indonesia and Belgium). \par \par \uc2  -Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030530dz5u00ane\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
