\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar crumbles as sentiment sours on U.S. economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2003\par 22:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 29 (Reuters) - The dollar fell broadly on Thursday, surrendering some of its three-day rally, after investors brooded on indications the U.S. job market remains weak. \par \par \uc2  The euro jumped to record highs against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 140.59 yen, and briefly recaptured the $1.19 mark against the dollar. The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now within striking distance of Tuesday's record high of $1.1932. \par \par \uc2  "The trend for a higher euro/dollar is still in place," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. "Until we see either strong U.S. equity performance or decent U.S. economic data, the euro is expected to trade higher." Mazanec said, adding that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could reach $1.20 during the next few sessions. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average fell 0.9 percent in late New York trade, weighing on the greenback. \par \par \uc2  After a delay, traders started to push the dollar lower in the wake of the weekly jobless report, which showed new claims for benefits held above 400,000, a level indicating slack employment. A 1.9 percent rise in first quarter U.S. gross domestic product matched low expectations and was seen as reflecting past performance. \par \par \uc2  ROUGH SLEDDING FOR DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  "After a nice correction in the last few days, I think the dollar faces some rough sledding over the next week as we look at both ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) policy and the U.S. employment report," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The market's focus has shifted to next week's employment report for May, expected to show the jobless rate rose to 6.1 percent from 6.0 in April. While Thursday's GDP report showed continued strong consumer spending, economists fret over how long that can last, given the weak employment situation. \par \par \uc2  "The data this morning led us to downgrade our payrolls number (forecast) for next week to a drop of 40,000 from 10,000. It's another reminder that the Fed is still out there and could cut rates," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York. \par \par \uc2  Another major focus is next week's ECB meeting and the expectation the bank will cut its benchmark lending rate from 2.50 percent, the latest Reuters poll of economists showed. \par \par \uc2  CURRENT ACCOUNT DEFICIT IRKS \par \par \uc2  While a cut would narrow the interest rate differential with the U.S. benchmark rate of 1.25 percent, giving investors less incentive to purchase euro-denominated assets, the record U.S. current account deficit may continue to drain demand for dollars, thereby lowering its value. \par \par \uc2  Canada's wider than expected current account surplus, reported on Thursday, drove the greenback sharply lower against its Canadian counterpart, underscoring the contrast with the hefty U.S. current account deficit, around 5 percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2  Late on Thursday in New York, the U.S. dollar was down 1.1 percent against the Canadian dollar, at C$1.3695. \par \par \uc2  The euro bounced back from session lows of $1.1692, jumping two cents higher to session peaks of $1.1904, up 1.15 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  Traders are on guard for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is committed to keeping the yen weak to protect its fragile export sector, one of the few bright spots in a gloomy economy. \par \par \uc2  At one point in the European trading session, the dollar rose to a four-week high of 119.15 yen before falling back to 118.24 yen in late trade, down 0.3 percent from Wednesday's New York close. Sterling surged to a four-month high of $1.6545. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the dollar was at 1.2846 Swiss francs, down 0.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030529dz5t01321\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
