\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar routed as rally stalls, off 1 pct vs euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2003\par 19:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 29 (Reuters) - The dollar fell broadly on Thursday, giving back some of a three-day rally, after investors digested news of still-weak U.S. employment and a small upward revision to first-quarter gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  Investors did not react to the data initially, analysts said, as the weekly jobless report showed new claims for benefits held above 400,000, a level indicating slack employment. The 1.9 percent GDP report was seen as uninspiring and reflecting past performance. \par \par \uc2  "After a nice correction in the last few days, I think the dollar faces some rough sledding over the next week as we look at both ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) policy and the U.S. employment report," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The market's focus has shifted to next week's employment report for May, expected to show the unemployment rate rose to 6.1 percent in May from 6.0 percent in April. While Thursday's GDP report continued to show strong consumer spending, economists are concerned over how long that can last, given the weak employment situation. \par \par \uc2  Another major focus is next week's ECB meeting and the expectation the bank will cut its benchmark lending rate from 2.50 percent, the latest Reuters poll of economists showed. \par \par \uc2  While a cut would narrow the interest rate differential with the U.S. benchmark rate of 1.25 percent, giving investors less incentive to purchase euro-denominated assets, the record U.S. current account deficit will continue to drain demand for dollars, thereby lowering its value. \par \par \uc2  "The market is focused on the deficits here, tainting the appeal of the dollar. You will see corrections from time to time, but there is not going to be a major rout in European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the time being," said David Schoenthal, senior managing director at Bear, Stearns & Co. Inc., in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rally stalled in early New York trade, prompting short-term investors to question whether the upward movement had come to an end and sparking a reversal. \par \par \uc2  "There is probably some disappointment in the market that the dollar did not strengthen further in this corrective move," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York. \par \par \uc2  "Generally speaking, the dollar is down across the board, and the data this morning led us to downgrade our payrolls number for next week to a drop of 40,000 from 10,000. It's another reminder that the Fed is still out there and could cut rates," he added. \par \par \uc2  The euro erased its losses against the dollar, having fallen as low as $1.1692, to surge two cents higher to $1.1904 , up 1.15 percent on the day. The euro is within striking distance of Tuesday's record high of $1.1932. \par \par \uc2  At one point in the European trading session, the dollar rose to a four-week high of 119.15 yen before falling back to 118.03 yen , off 0.45 percent from Wednesday's New York close. \par \par \uc2  Investors are on guard for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is committed to keeping the yen weak in order to protect its fragile export sector, one of the few bright spots in a gloomy economy. \par \par \uc2  The euro moved further into record territory against the yen, trading as high as 140.59 yen , up 0.78 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid to 1.2840 Swiss francs , off nearly 1 percent in value. Sterling surged to a four-month high of $1.6545 , a gain of 0.85 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030529dz5t00yf5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
