\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar touches 4-week high vs yen, euro at bay. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2003\par 10:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 29 (Reuters) - The dollar touched a four-week high against the yen and held on to the previous session's sharp gains against the euro on Thursday as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued its pause after a breakneck rally. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said recent dollar selling had become overstretched and the greenback's snap back from this week's record low on the euro did not mean it had pulled out of its longer-term decline. \par \par \uc2  "The underlying picture for the dollar has not changed much. All the market's telling you is that we have moved too far, too fast and there is room to pause and take stock," said Jim McCormick, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "In that context dollar/yen has become a major focus. We've had a slew of modestly bad data out of Japan this week and the Bank of Japan is getting a lot of help in getting dollar/yen back to a level it feels comfortable with." \par \par \uc2  By 0730 GMT the dollar was a third of a percent up on the day at 118.93 yen after hitting a peak at 119.10 earlier, buoyed by traders' wariness of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb any signs of export-damaging strength in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was also a third of a percent up on the euro at $1.1720, two cents above Tuesday's record low. \par \par \uc2  The yen was slightly weaker on the euro at 139.67 yen but up from a record low set in Asian trade around 139.85. \par \par \uc2  RIDING A WAVE \par \par \uc2  The greenback has gained strength against the yen due to concerns the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen on behalf of the Finance Ministry. Some dealers suspect the authorities, concerned yen strength will dampen exports and harm the stagnant economy, stepped in on Tuesday, when the dollar fell to around 116.20 yen in post-Asian trade. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the past two weeks, and this was thought to be stopping the dollar from falling below 115 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I think a dollar-buying mood spread in the market once it rose beyond 118 yen," said Gen Kawabe, manager of the treasury department at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  Traders said however Japanese exporters then took advantage of the higher levels to sell dollars for yen, which took the shine off the greenback. \par \par \uc2  For the euro, the market's focus was shifting to next Thursday's European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting to see if the ECB will cut interest rates from 2.5 percent currently. \par \par \uc2  ECB chief economist Otmar Issing said on Wednesday a strong euro helped tame inflation and gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  room to move on interest rates, fuelling market expectations of a cut possibly as big as half a percentage point. \par \par \uc2  Politicians in Belgium and Germany also joined recent calls for the bank to cut rates. \par \par \uc2  In the United States, San Francisco Federal Reserve President Robert Parry, a voting member on the Fed's policy making committee, said late on Wednesday the economy was still stuck in a soft patch. \par \par \uc2  He said the Fed still had room to cut rates in spite of forecasts of stronger growth because the pace of growth would not be fast enough to use up spare capacity in the economy. \par \par \uc2  U.S. data due at 1230 GMT includes weekly jobless claims which are expected to have fallen to 419,000 in the week ended May 24, from 428,000 a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Commerce Department's second estimate of first-quarter growth is forecast to show the economy expanded 1.9 percent in the period. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030529dz5t00g29\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
