\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar extends gains, hits 4-week high vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2003\par 04:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 29 (Reuters) - The dollar rose against the yen and euro on Thursday, hitting a four-week high against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the euro fell prey to profit-taking after jumping to record peaks this week. \par \par \uc2  Traders also said the dollar gained because Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was finally having an effect. \par \par \uc2  But most traders said there were no fundamental reasons behind the recent recovery in the dollar and many remained sceptical over the outlook for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Many hedge funds were basically long on U.S. Treasuries and gold and short on U.S. stocks and the dollar," said Mitsuo Imaizumi, deputy general manager of the international bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  department at Daiwa Securities SMBC. \par \par \uc2  "What we are seeing now is the unwinding of those positions." \par \par \uc2  At 0216 GMT, the dollar was trading around 118.97 yen versus 118.67 in late U.S. trade. It briefly rose as high as 119.12 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1741 compared with $1.1755. It hit a record high of around $1.1935 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, it was at 139.68 yen versus 139.52 in late New York and a record high of 139.85 reached earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  The greenback has turned solid amid concerns over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some dealers suspect the authorities stepped in on Tuesday, when the dollar fell to around 116.20 yen in post-Asian trade. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the past two weeks, and this was thought to be stopping the dollar from falling below 115 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I think Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are finally showing some effect," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing at UFJ Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Having seen the dollar break through 118 yen, there should be more demand to buy the dollar back," Sahara said. \par \par \uc2  For the euro, the market's focus was shifting to next Thursday's European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting to see if it will cut interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "I think a rate cut by 0.25 percentage point is already factored in," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the interbank desk at Trust and Custody Services Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But traders have been split over whether a rate cut would weigh on the euro, which has been favoured over the dollar due to its higher interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Some traders argue that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would gain from a rate cut since such a move would likely help the economy. \par \par \uc2  Besides the ECB, market focus is on Friday's final readings of May consumer confidence from the University of Michigan and manufacturing activity in the Midwest. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030529dz5t00e3c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
