\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar buoyant after euro's pause, Japanese action. \b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2003\par 01:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 29 (Reuters) - The dollar retained sharp gains across the board in early Thursday trade as the euro consolidated after hitting a record high earlier in the week and rounds of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  finally showed some effect, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  After the euro's surge of about eight percent in the last month and more than 14 percent since the start of the year, it was time for consolidation, they said, with speculation of a possible interest cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heating up. \par \par \uc2  But the market is not overly convinced that the dollar will regain bullishness, as a series of economic indicators in the United States is giving a mixed picture of the economy, and there is also speculation of a U.S. credit easing. \par \par \uc2  "It's much too early to say the dollar's outlook has turned bright again despite seeing it recover sharply yesterday," said a trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "Although technically the dollar looks good now, we've also seen poor U.S. durable good figures," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. durable goods orders fell a sharp 2.4 percent in April, a bigger drop than economists had expected. \par \par \uc2  But the Conference Board said earlier in the week that U.S. consumer confidence rose in May to a six-month high, and April U.S. new home sales rose 1.7 percent, more than expected. \par \par \uc2  At 2328 GMT, the dollar was quoted at $1.1752/60 against the euro, compared with $1.1755/61. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit a record high of around $1.1935 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also rose to a three-week high of 118.94 yen in post-Asian trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's upward momentum picked up speed after a break through key resistance around 118.10 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has turned solid amid concerns over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with some dealers recently suspecting that the authorities may have stepped in on Tuesday, when the dollar fell to around 116.20 yen in post-Asian trading. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily the past two weeks, which was believed to be protecting the dollar from falling below 115 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I think Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are finally showing some effect," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group at UFJ Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Having seen the dollar break through 118 yen, there should be more demand to buy the dollar back," Sahara said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar could head towards 120 yen if its upward momentum is increased by a possible ECB credit easing next week, Sahara said. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at 139.32/35 yen after hitting a record high of 139.73 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Beside the ECB, the market focus is on Friday's final readings of May consumer confidence from the University of Michigan and manufacturing activity in the Midwest, with the market also closely watching Thursday's weekly jobless claims. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030528dz5s018r6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
