\par\par \b \uc2  Euro steady but off highs as focus shifts to ECB. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2003\par 04:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 28 (Reuters) - The euro steadied on Wednesday after rising to a record high against the dollar the previous day, with the market focus shifting to a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy-setting meeting next week. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen over eight percent in the last month. It hit a record high in Tokyo on Tuesday then extended its gains to around $1.1935 in New York. \par \par \uc2  "Moves were quite steep yesterday so the market is being hesitant to break above the closely watched $1.2 mark now," said Koichi Abe, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading manager at Aozora Bank. \par \par \uc2  At 0204 GMT in Tokyo the euro was at $1.1825 versus $1.1812 in late New York. Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro was trading at 138.53 yen compared to a U.S. close of 138.48 yen. It hit a record high of 139.17 yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was steady at 117.15 yen against 117.25 yen in late New York. \par \par \uc2  Though fundamental factors supporting the euro remain unchanged, such as interest rate differences and a current account surplus in the eurozone compared with a deficit in the United States, dealers were cautious about the speed of its rise. \par \par \uc2  "I think we've seen the short-term peak for now," said Kenji Kobayashi, manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Traders were looking ahead to the ECB meeting on June 5 to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut interest rates, which some believe could weigh on the euro. \par \par \uc2  But others said a possible rate cut would be a supporting factor for the euro. \par \par \uc2  "I think they will cut rates but I think this will help the euro," said Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Kobayashi. "If they cut, it could help the European economy." \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR/YEN RATE SEEN CONFINED \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, many traders expect the dollar to stay confined to its current levels against the yen, with its upside capped by likely selling by Japanese exporters and its downside supported by fears of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said the U.S. economy was showing signs of improvement and that this should help to stabilise the market. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi, the vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters he hoped the market would reflect improvement in the U.S. economy, meaning a rise in the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  According to data released on Tuesday, U.S. consumer confidence rose in May to a six-month high, while April U.S. new home sales rose 1.7 percent, more than expected. \par \par \uc2  Existing home sales last month climbed to an annualised rate of 5.84 million, also more than expected. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030528dz5s0059a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
