\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Ends Higher vs. Euro, Yen, Pound \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2 1,075 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2003\par 23:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar advanced Wednesday as investors continued to book profits after the euro's recent leap and shunned the yen for higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While dropping back significantly from Tuesday's all-time high against the dollar, the common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared to a fresh historical peak versus the yen. Given the euro's correction and the yen's drop across the board, the dollar pulled ahead against its major and secondary rivals. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the dollar was trading at 118.65 yen, up  from 117.21 yen Tuesday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.1765, down from $1.1830 a day earlier and well off its all-time high. However, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was continuing to trade in high ranges versus the yen after hitting an all-time high of 139.74 yen earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  In trading that reflected the dollar's firmer tone, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 1.2955 Swiss francs, up from 1.2864 francs late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.6370, down from $1.6381 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Although the greenback enjoyed a rebound Wednesday, some analysts feel it may prove to be a short-term phenomenon. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment one has to say it's just a pause [in the euro's rise], but that could have quite a bit of downside as the climb has been quite steep," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Mellon Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar largely ignored disappointing durable-goods data which showed that April orders for big-ticket items fell by 2.4% to $168.93 billion, the largest monthly decline since last September. \par \par \uc2  Many in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets believe the dollar is set to continue to decline - depressed by widening U.S. budget and current account deficits, a more sluggish U.S. economy and a sense that the Bush administration quietly supports a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it helps boost profits at large American corporations. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are now paying close attention to some of the greenback's more secondary rivals for indications of broader based dollar relief yet to come, even if it proves temporary. \par \par \uc2  In particular, dips in the Canadian, New Zealand and Australian dollars have made investors cautious that after the euro's extreme march higher, any correction now could be more brutal than many expect. \par \par \uc2  "Earlier this year, it was the commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that tended to lead the move against the dollar," said Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with BNP Paribas in New York. "If they are correcting against the dollar, then other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may follow." \par \par \uc2  The Canadian, New Zealand and Australian dollars are referred to as commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because their economies rely heavily on the export of commodities. Late Wednesday afternoon, the U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3832, up from C$1.3768 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  After hitting a fresh 41-month high of 66.25 U.S. cents Tuesday, the Australian dollar fell Wednesday to its lowest level since May 16, scraping 64.66 U.S. cents before trading at around 65.10 U.S. cents midafternoon in New York, down from 66.55 U.S. cents a day earlier. The New Zealand dollar was fetching 57.73 U.S. cents, down from 58.14 U.S. cents a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Losses in these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  largely mirrored moves in  the euro. From its all-time high Tuesday around $1.1930 to its session lows Wednesday at about $1.1710, the European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed about two cents on what strategists largely attributed to investors booking profits on the belief that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut its short-term interest rate at its next meeting on June 5. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark rate of 2.50% is double its U.S. equivalent at 1.25%, a factor often cited as partially behind the euro's strength. A cut in European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates would dull the euro's luster, at least in the short term, analysts argue. \par \par \uc2  Interest-rate differentials have been a major attraction for the dollar bloc \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well. Canada's benchmark rate is currently at 3.25% while Australia enjoys a 3.5 percentage-point advantage over the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Having previously rallied at 15% against the greenback since the beginning of the year, the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lately suffered from the outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome, the discovery of one case of mad-cow disease and worries that a slump in inflation may lead to a lowering of interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The greenback has shattered resistance levels against its Canadian cousin in recent days, heading to a high of C$1.3922 on Wednesday from Tuesday's close of C$1.3770 and last week's low of $1.3674. Late Wednesday, the U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3851. \par \par \uc2  In a shift of focus, some argue interest-rate differentials may have helped the dollar against the yen, with the 10-year Japanese government bond yield dropping to 0.525%, a record low, while the 10-year Treasury note was yielding 3.41% late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "That's really aiding the dollar across the board," said Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the dollar against the yen has also been giving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a boost in recent weeks. Traders believe the Bank of Japan - acting on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - now has the market on the run after it successfully pushed the dollar above Tuesday's low of 116.23 yen to Wednesday's high around 118.91 yen. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government has repeatedly stated that it wants to keep the yen from strengthening too much as it would endanger the Japanese economy's chances of an export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  Estimates of recent Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been massive. After buying an average of $7 billion per month for the first four months of the year, strategists estimate dollar purchases by the Bank of Japan since May 8 are $20 billion to $30 billion. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2248 \par \par \uc2  michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Agnes Crane contributed to this story.) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  05-28-03 1753ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030528dz5s005k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
