\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro tags record high vs dollar before retreat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2003\par 19:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 27 (Reuters) - The euro tagged another record high against the dollar on Tuesday as demand for higher-yielding euro-based assets overshadowed solid U.S. economic data. \par \par \uc2  But the euro's advance was halted by investors booking profits after a 2.7 percent ascent against the greenback over the last four sessions. \par \par \uc2  "People are taking a breather on the dollar selling, with a little profit-taking against the euro and yen after making the new highs on the data this morning," said Russell LaScala, chief spot dealer at Deutsche Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consumer confidence rose in May, but less than expected, while low mortgage rates spurred home buying in April, reports showed on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  "The low interest rates that are going to help the housing market is not something that is going to be a factor in helping lift the dollar," said Steven Englander, chief North American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in New York. \par \par \uc2  "What the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would want in order to buy dollars are data to suggest within a reasonable time that the Fed is going to raise interest rates in light of a stronger economy," said Englander, who said these data were not going to change anyone's view that the dollar will remain weak. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit a record high $1.1932 in a knee-jerk sell-off after the U.S. data was released before dropping back to $1.1867, slightly below Monday's New York close. The euro first traded, at $1.1747, on Jan. 1, 1999. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consumer confidence rose in May to a six-month high of 83.8 but below economists' forecasts of 84.9. \par \par \uc2  While rising consumer confidence is welcomed, it is not a component of gross domestic product, whereas new home sales are. \par \par \uc2  April U.S. new home sales rose 1.7 percent, more than expected, while existing home sales last month climbed to an annualized rate of 5.84 million, also more than expected. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said there was no specific factor driving the euro's gains but that it was aided generally by investor demand for euro zone government bonds and a lack of objection to its strengthening from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The ECB meets next week to decide if it will cut its benchmark interest rate of 2.50 percent, which could spur borrowing and investment in the euro zone's anemic economy. \par \par \uc2  Even if the interest rate differential with the United States narrows - the benchmark interest rate is 1.25 percent - the dollar still faces the pressure of a steady outflow of cash due to the record U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  current account data showed investors poured money into the euro zone in March at double the rate seen in February, with the portfolio investment inflow climbing to 20.7 billion euros from 10.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  Since the start of 2003 the euro is up roughly 13 percent on the dollar and 11 percent on the yen. \par \par \uc2  Against Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro reached a record 139.13 yen before falling back to 138.88 yen, up 0.13 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly slid to a one-week low against the Japanese yen of 116.21 before ricocheting back to 116.98 yen, up 0.12 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they saw big orders for dollar purchases from Japanese banks come into the market, consistent with a pattern of dollar/yen buying at these levels that has been attributed to covert yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Investors kept in mind Tuesday's warning from Japan's top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi that Japan would act to prevent excessive swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  The greenback fell versus the Swiss franc which pushed to a fresh four-and-half-year peak of 1.2786 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Sterling rose to a four-month high of $1.6471 and a three-month high of 72.14 pence per euro after the U.S. data. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030527dz5r00y3v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
