\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Buying Helps Slow Dollar Slide \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 856 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2003\par 21:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Agnes T. Crane \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Bludgeoned by sellers from most corners of the world, the dollar may have averted complete meltdown thanks to Asian central banks. \par \par \uc2  Japan and China, in particular, are still gobbling up the greenback - despite the pervasive negative sentiment in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, in an attempt to navigate their economies through the global economic slump, strategists say. \par \par \uc2  In Japan's case, a weak yen is its goal. After muddling through more than 10-years of sluggish growth, the Japanese financial authorities hope to export their economy back onto solid footing on the back of weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar's broad-based declines against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , most notably the euro, has forced Japan to buy dollars in recent weeks, purchases that have tempered the impact of rampant selling by other investors. Since the begiining of 2003, Japanese monetary authorities have favored covert approach to dollar buying rather than direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand for USD out of Asian central banks to combat strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains the only real curb to a disorderly USD selloff at this stage," said Daniel Katzive, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in Stanford, Conn, in a research note. \par \par \uc2  Weakening domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the only reason behind appetite for dollars by some Asian central banks. For China, it's a result of its fixed exchange rate. China pegs the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, to the dollar, allowing it to trade in a very narrow band. \par \par \uc2  Due to government rules on trade, mainland Chinese exporters are not permitted to hold onto dollar revenues and, therefore, must exchange their greenbacks for yuan with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A surge in Chinese exports has brought a deluge of dollars into the government's coffers, which has helped keep the dollar firm. China holds over $300 billion in reserves, topped only by Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Between the Japanese managing of dollar/yen and fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate in China, these two things are generating a large amount of dollar buying," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics in Essex, Conn., who also argues that this buying has helped temper the dollar selloff. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    But Asian Wall May Exacerbate Dollar Losses Elsewhere\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ongoing dollar purchases by Asian central banks have helped prop up\par the greenback against their regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but others say that it\par has done little to stem the selling against the euro and the Swiss\par franc. Some strategists even argue that it has exaggerated the euro's\par climb to record territory since the dollar cannot sufficiently\par depreciate elsewhere.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro broke new ground this week, reaching $1.1931 in intraday\par trading Tuesday before falling back below $1.1900, as the familiar\par themes of interest rate differentials and current account concerns\par continued to hammer the greenback.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the euro has climbed approximately 14% against the dollar since\par the beginning of the year, the dollar has only weakened 2.1% against the\par yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tim Stewart, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York, said\par the downward momentum has been "a pretty powerful driver of dollar\par weakness" since the Federal Reserve signaled the market that low\par interest rates are here to stay for some time. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which acts on behalf of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has "only helped" the euro's ascension, said\par Stewart.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A low yield environment has caused some investors to channel their\par funds to areas such as the eurozone, Australia, New Zealand and Canada\par where interest rates are higher. The flows, or the perception of such\par flows, has helped weaken the dollar and strengthen other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Deep Pockets In Japan To Support Dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While Japan's economy has stumbled through the last decade, slipping\par occasionally into recession, the nation continues to run a huge current\par account surplus. This financial cushion gives authorities there plenty\par of maneuvering room to keep covert \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  going.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They can continue indefinitely as long as people have faith in Bank\par of Japan and essentially the government," said Gilmore. If the public,\par however, begins to question Japan's holdings of domestic bonds as well\par as its vast investments in U.S. Treasurys, this could change, he said,\par adding that it's not likely.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market participants believe that the BOJ uses the dollars it purchases\par in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to buy U.S. Treasurys. In fact, Federal\par Reserve custody holdings of U.S. securities on behalf of foreign central\par banks for the week ended May 21, reported a $20 billion surge compared\par with an average increase of $2.5 billion over the past year, according\par to strategists.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The $20 billion in securities included $18 billion in U.S. Treasurys\par and $1.4 billion in agencies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This Friday, the BOJ will release data that will reveal the extent of\par its covert \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this month. Traders have estimate that the MOF\par ordered the BOJ to sell upwards of Y4 trillion in the last several weeks\par to curb the yen's rise.\par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122;\par  agnes.crane@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030527dz5r00175\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
