\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options:Euro/Dlr Volatilities Rise After Euro Climb \b0\par\par\uc2 511 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2003\par 18:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month euro/dollar and dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were mixed Friday in New York, reflecting the euro's dramatic spike against the dollar as well as stubborn Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that shored up the greenback against the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  The four-year-old European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged to four-year highs of $1.1807 against the dollar in Friday's session, and it broke its historical high against the Japanese yen by climbing to Y138.12. It also scored its biggest gains against the Swiss franc since Sept. 11, 2001, topping off at CHF1.5236. Meanwhile, the dollar fell against the same \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There doesn't seem to be any strong fundamental news that could provide the dollar with strong support (against the euro)," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Putnam Investments in Boston, echoing trader comments that the euro would likely break higher when trading resumed in earnest on Tuesday and serve as the main impetus for the spike in euro/dollar volatilities. \par \par \uc2  Around noon EDT (1600 GMT), the dollar was trading at Y116.82, down from Y117.22 late Thursday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.17827, up dramatically from $1.1699 late Thursday. The dollar was at CHF1.2928, down from CHF1.2949 late Thursday. Sterling was at $1.6371, up from $1.6352. \par \par \uc2  Traders ascribed the lack of a precipitous dollar fall against the yen and the decrease in dollar/yen volatility to further aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the greenback continued to trade above the Y116.50 level after falling back to near Y115 early in the week. \par \par \uc2  Although the amount that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent this week hasn't been confirmed, estimates of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  vary from about Y2.5 trillion to as high as Y4 trillion. \par \par \uc2  Next week, the Bank of Japan is expected to issue its own reserves data that should give a more accurate estimate of how much it has spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the course of the month. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 8.70%-9.00%, lower from 8.85%-9.10% earlier in Tokyo. One-month euro/dollar option volatilities were at 11.00%-11.30%, up from 10.80%-11.10% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favor dollar puts at -1.60% to -1.30%. Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro calls are favored against the dollar by 0.80%-1.10%. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248; michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 44427 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030523dz5n0011z\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0